var PRIMERS=[

{cat:"Burgundy",
lead:"One grape for each colour and a thousand named plots: Burgundy is Pinot Noir and Chardonnay read through place. The Côte d'Or runs from Dijon to Maranges, the Côte de Nuits chiefly red and the Côte de Beaune red and white, with Chablis alone to the north and the Côte Chalonnaise, the Mâconnais and Beaujolais to the south.",
exam:"The four-tier pyramid (regional, village, Premier Cru, Grand Cru), which villages hold which famous Grands Crus, Chablis and its Kimmeridgian soil, the Chalonnaise and Mâconnais villages, and Gamay's home in Beaujolais with its ten crus.",
facts:[
["The pyramid","Regional Bourgogne (with Bourgogne Aligoté, Coteaux Bourguignons, Crémant de Bourgogne and, since 2017, Bourgogne Côte d'Or), then village (Gevrey-Chambertin, Meursault), then Premier Cru (the village and a named climat), then Grand Cru, where the climat alone stands on the label: Chambertin, Montrachet, La Tâche. There are 33 Grands Crus, Chablis Grand Cru counted as one, together a tiny fraction of the region's wine."],
["The slope","The Côte d'Or is a narrow escarpment of Jurassic limestone and marl facing east and south-east. The Grands Crus sit mostly mid-slope, with village land on the flatter ground below. A climat is a named plot with its own character; a monopole is a vineyard owned whole by one estate, as Romanée-Conti and La Tâche are."],
["Côte de Nuits","Grand Cru red country, north to south: Gevrey-Chambertin (nine Grands Crus, Chambertin and Chambertin-Clos de Bèze at their head), Morey-Saint-Denis (Clos de la Roche, Clos de Tart), Chambolle-Musigny (Musigny, Bonnes-Mares), Vougeot (Clos de Vougeot), Flagey-Échezeaux (Échezeaux, Grands-Échezeaux), Vosne-Romanée (Romanée-Conti, La Tâche, Richebourg) and Nuits-Saint-Georges, which has none."],
["Côte de Beaune","Corton is its only red Grand Cru. The whites are the glory: Corton-Charlemagne on the same hill, and Montrachet, shared by Puligny and Chassagne, with its satellites Chevalier-, Bâtard-, Bienvenues-Bâtard- and Criots-Bâtard-Montrachet. Volnay and Pommard make great reds and Meursault great whites, all with Premier Cru as their ceiling."],
["Chablis","Chardonnay alone on Kimmeridgian marl and limestone full of tiny fossil oysters: steely, saline, often unoaked. Four tiers: Petit Chablis (mostly on the younger Portlandian limestone), Chablis, Chablis Premier Cru and Chablis Grand Cru, whose seven climats share one south-west-facing slope across the Serein from the town: Blanchot, Bougros, Les Clos (the most famous), Grenouilles, Preuses, Valmur and Vaudésir."],
["Beaujolais","Gamay, the best of it from the granite of the north, often made by carbonic or semi-carbonic maceration. Three tiers: Beaujolais, Beaujolais-Villages and the ten crus, whose labels lead with the cru's own name: Saint-Amour, Juliénas, Chénas, Moulin-à-Vent, Fleurie, Chiroubles, Morgon, Régnié, Brouilly and Côte de Brouilly. Beaujolais Nouveau is released on the third Thursday of November."],
["Côte Chalonnaise","Burgundy's value shelf, with five village names: Bouzeron (Aligoté's own village appellation), Rully (white, red and much Crémant de Bourgogne), Mercurey and Givry (chiefly Pinot Noir) and Montagny (Chardonnay only)."],
["Mâconnais","Chardonnay country, warmer and rounder: Mâcon-Villages, Viré-Clessé, Saint-Véran and Pouilly-Fuissé, which gained Premier Cru climats from the 2020 vintage."],
["Négociant and domaine","A domaine bottles wine from its own vineyards; a négociant (Jadot, Drouhin, Bouchard Père et Fils, Faiveley) buys grapes, must or wine, and the great houses own vineyards too. Both make benchmark Burgundy, and 'Domaine' or 'Maison' on the label is the clue to which you hold."]],
traps:[
"Pouilly-Fuissé is Mâconnais Chardonnay; Pouilly-Fumé is Loire Sauvignon Blanc.",
"Nuits-Saint-Georges, Volnay, Pommard and Meursault have no Grand Cru: famous villages with a Premier Cru ceiling.",
"Gevrey-Chambertin is a village wine and Chambertin the Grand Cru: villages added their most famous vineyard to their names, so the longer name is the lesser wine.",
"Musigny is the Côte de Nuits Grand Cru known for a white as well as its red; Corton is the Côte de Beaune's only red Grand Cru.",
"Chablis Grand Cru is one slope of seven climats: 'Les Clos' is a climat, not a village."]},

{cat:"Bordeaux",
lead:"A maritime blending region cut by the Gironde and its two rivers: Cabernet Sauvignon leads on the gravel of the Left Bank, Merlot on the clay and limestone of the Right. The 1855 Classification fixed the Médoc's order; Saint-Émilion revises its own; Pomerol has never had one.",
exam:"The 1855 First Growths and their communes, the Médoc communes' signatures, the Right Bank icons (Pétrus, Cheval Blanc, Ausone), Graves and Pessac-Léognan, Sauternes and noble rot, Entre-Deux-Mers, and the permitted grapes.",
facts:[
["1855 First Growths","Lafite Rothschild, Latour and Mouton Rothschild in Pauillac, Margaux in Margaux, and Haut-Brion in Pessac-Léognan, the one from Graves. Mouton was raised from Second Growth in 1973, the one promotion ever made. The red list holds 61 châteaux in five growths, ranked in 1855 by price. All five First Growths are Left Bank."],
["Médoc communes","Saint-Estèphe: sturdy, on cooler clay. Pauillac: three First Growths, cassis-and-cedar Cabernet. Saint-Julien: the balanced middle, with no First Growth. Margaux: the perfumed one, with the most classified estates. Listrac and Moulis, inland, hold no classified growths."],
["Right Bank","Merlot with Cabernet Franc. Saint-Émilion, on limestone and clay, was classified in 1955 and is revised about once a decade; Ausone and Cheval Blanc, then Angélus, withdrew before the 2022 revision, which named Figeac and Pavie its two Premiers Grands Crus Classés A. Pomerol, on clay and gravel, has no classification: Pétrus and Le Pin stand unranked."],
["Graves and Pessac-Léognan","Home of Haut-Brion, on the gravel soils that gave Graves its name. Its classification of 1953, revised in 1959, is the one that covers both red and dry white, and every classified estate lies within Pessac-Léognan, an appellation since 1987. Smoke, gravel, warm stones."],
["Sauternes and Barsac","Sémillon, thin-skinned and prone to botrytis, with Sauvignon Blanc and a little Muscadelle. Autumn mists rise where the cold Ciron meets the warmer Garonne and breed noble rot. Barsac may sell under its own name or as Sauternes. d'Yquem stands alone as Premier Cru Supérieur in the 1855 list of sweet wines."],
["Entre-Deux-Mers","Between the Garonne and the Dordogne. Long a dry white appellation, mostly Sauvignon Blanc and Sémillon, it has also covered red since the 2023 vintage, under stricter rules of its own (Merlot, the two Cabernets, Malbec and Petit Verdot, denser planting and longer ageing before release); reds of the same land that do not meet them still sell as Bordeaux or Bordeaux Supérieur."],
["The grapes","Red: Cabernet Sauvignon, Merlot, Cabernet Franc, Petit Verdot, Malbec and Carménère. White: Sémillon, Sauvignon Blanc, Muscadelle. Since 2021, Bordeaux and Bordeaux Supérieur may also use a few heat-tolerant newcomers, such as Marselan and Touriga Nacional, up to a tenth of the blend."],
["The system","Nearly everything is château-bottled and blended. 'Grand Vin' is unregulated, while 'Grand Cru Classé' means an actual classification: know which one. In Saint-Émilion, 'Grand Cru' alone is an appellation, not a rank."]],
traps:[
"Haut-Brion is an 1855 First Growth from Graves, not the Médoc.",
"Pomerol has no classification at all: Pétrus is unranked.",
"Sauternes must be sweet; a dry white from the same zone is sold as Bordeaux Blanc.",
"Carménère and Malbec are legal Bordeaux varieties: historic, now minor.",
"Saint-Émilion Grand Cru is an appellation; Grand Cru Classé and Premier Grand Cru Classé are the classification."]},

{cat:"Champagne",
lead:"The world's benchmark sparkling wine: Chardonnay, Pinot Noir and Meunier from chalk at France's cold northern edge, made by the traditional method (the méthode champenoise), with the second fermentation in the bottle it is sold in, then ageing on the lees, riddling, disgorgement and dosage.",
exam:"The traditional method's steps in order, the sweetness levels, the styles (Blanc de Blancs, Blanc de Noirs, rosé), the subregions and their grapes, the ageing minimums, the label codes and the prestige cuvées.",
facts:[
["The method, in order","Base wines blended (assemblage); the tirage, wine with sugar and yeast (liqueur de tirage), starts a second fermentation in bottle; ageing on the lees; riddling (remuage) gathers the sediment in the neck; disgorgement (dégorgement) removes it; dosage (liqueur d'expédition) sets the sweetness; then cork and wire cage (muselet)."],
["Ageing minimums","Non-vintage: 15 months from tirage, 12 of them on the lees. Vintage: three years. The great houses go well beyond both."],
["Sweetness ladder","In grams of sugar a litre: Brut Nature (under 3, no dosage), Extra Brut (up to 6), Brut (under 12), Extra Dry (12 to 17), Sec (17 to 32), Demi-Sec (32 to 50), Doux (over 50). Extra Dry is sweeter than Brut."],
["Styles","Blanc de Blancs: white grapes only, almost always Chardonnay. Blanc de Noirs: white wine from black grapes. Rosé: by blending red and white wine (allowed here) or by saignée."],
["Subregions","Montagne de Reims: Pinot Noir. Vallée de la Marne: Meunier. Côte des Blancs: Chardonnay on the purest chalk. Côte des Bar, in the Aube: Pinot Noir on Kimmeridgian marl, nearer Chablis than Reims. Seventeen villages are rated Grand Cru and some forty Premier Cru."],
["The grapes","Chardonnay, Pinot Noir and Meunier make almost all of it; Pinot Blanc, Pinot Gris, Arbane and Petit Meslier are legal but rare. The still wines are Coteaux Champenois and the rosé of Les Riceys, Rosé des Riceys."],
["Who made it","The label code says: NM (négociant-manipulant, a house), RM (récoltant-manipulant, a grower), CM (a co-operative), RC (a co-operative member's own label), SR (a growers' company), ND (a négociant-distributeur) and MA (marque d'acheteur, a buyer's own brand)."],
["Prestige cuvées","Moët's Dom Pérignon, Louis Roederer's Cristal, Taittinger's Comtes de Champagne, Pol Roger's Sir Winston Churchill, Veuve Clicquot's La Grande Dame; Krug makes only prestige wines, its multi-vintage Grande Cuvée at the core."],
["Pressure and bottles","About 5 to 6 atmospheres behind the cork. Half bottle 37.5 cl, bottle 75 cl, Magnum 1.5 litres (thought to age best), then Jeroboam 3, Rehoboam 4.5, Methuselah 6, Salmanazar 9, Balthazar 12 and Nebuchadnezzar 15: the biblical ladder is a favourite question."]],
traps:[
"Extra Dry reads drier than it is: it sits sweeter than Brut.",
"Meunier is a legal, major grape: candidates forget the third variety.",
"Rosé by blending red and white wine is allowed in Champagne; EU rules forbid it for still rosé.",
"Grower Champagne carries 'RM' (récoltant-manipulant) on the label; houses carry 'NM'.",
"The Côte des Bar lies in the Aube, far south of Reims and Épernay, and its soil is marl, not chalk."]},

{cat:"Loire",
lead:"France's longest river carries a cool, white-led spine from Muscadet's Atlantic salt to Sancerre's flint: Melon de Bourgogne, Chenin Blanc, Sauvignon Blanc and Cabernet Franc, in every style from bone dry to botrytised to sparkling.",
exam:"Which grape belongs to which appellation (Muscadet and Melon; Vouvray and Chenin; Sancerre, Pouilly-Fumé and Sauvignon Blanc; Chinon, Bourgueil, Saumur-Champigny and Cabernet Franc), sur lie, the sweet Chenin zones and the rosés.",
facts:[
["Pays Nantais","Muscadet, the best of it Muscadet Sèvre et Maine, from Melon de Bourgogne. 'Sur lie' wines rest on their lees through the winter until bottling, for texture and a faint prickle: the oyster wine. Crus communaux such as Clisson and Gorges age far longer on the lees."],
["Anjou-Saumur","Chenin Blanc at its centre: dry Savennières, with Coulée de Serrant, a single-estate appellation within it; sweet, botrytised Coteaux du Layon, with Quarts de Chaume (the Loire's only Grand Cru) and Bonnezeaux within it. Saumur-Champigny is Cabernet Franc. Rosé d'Anjou is off-dry, mostly Grolleau; Cabernet d'Anjou is off-dry rosé from the Cabernets."],
["Touraine","Vouvray and Montlouis-sur-Loire: Chenin in every sweetness (sec, demi-sec, moelleux) and sparkling. Chinon, Bourgueil and Saint-Nicolas-de-Bourgueil: Cabernet Franc, with raspberry, pencil shavings and green pepper. Cour-Cheverny is Romorantin's own appellation."],
["Centre","Sancerre and Pouilly-Fumé: Sauvignon Blanc on limestone, marl and flint (silex), the flint lending smoke. Sancerre also makes red and rosé from Pinot Noir. Menetou-Salon, Quincy and Reuilly are the value ring; Pouilly-sur-Loire is the rare Chasselas."],
["Crémant de Loire","Traditional-method sparkling, mostly Chenin. Saumur is its capital, and the soft tuffeau caves there age millions of bottles."],
["Structure signature","High acid everywhere: a cool, northerly climate, Atlantic at the river's mouth and more continental upstream. Even the reds run light, cool and crunchy."]],
traps:[
"Pouilly-Fumé (Loire Sauvignon Blanc) against Pouilly-Fuissé (Mâconnais Chardonnay): the exam's favourite trip wire.",
"Muscadet's grape is Melon de Bourgogne, not 'Muscadet' and not Muscat.",
"Savennières is dry Chenin; Coteaux du Layon is sweet: the same grape, opposite intent.",
"Sancerre rouge is Pinot Noir, not Cabernet Franc.",
"Rosé d'Anjou and Cabernet d'Anjou are off-dry; Rosé de Loire must be dry."]},

{cat:"Alsace",
lead:"German-sounding grapes under French law in the rain shadow of the Vosges: dry, aromatic, varietally labelled whites from Riesling, Gewurztraminer, Pinot Gris and Muscat, the four 'noble' grapes that hold almost all of the 51 Grands Crus.",
exam:"The four noble grapes, varietal labelling (unusual in French AOP), the Grand Cru rules and their recent exceptions, Vendanges Tardives and Sélection de Grains Nobles, and Crémant d'Alsace.",
facts:[
["The noble four","Riesling, Gewurztraminer, Pinot Gris and Muscat are the Grand Cru grapes. The exceptions are few: Sylvaner in Zotzenberg, blends in Altenberg de Bergheim and Kaefferkopf, and now Pinot Noir in three Grands Crus (Hengst and Kirchberg de Barr from the 2022 vintage, Vorbourg by a decree of 2024)."],
["Labelling","Alsace labels by variety, and a named grape means all of that grape, in the tall, slim flûte bottle the rules require: France's clearest labels. Edelzwicker and Gentil are the blends."],
["The other grapes","Pinot Blanc (often blended with Auxerrois and still sold as Pinot Blanc), Sylvaner, and Pinot Noir, the one red, once light and now more and more serious."],
["Styles","Traditionally dry and full-bodied. Vendanges Tardives: late harvest, rich, often off-dry. Sélection de Grains Nobles: botrytised and unmistakably sweet. Both come only from the four noble grapes."],
["Climate","Colmar lies in one of France's driest spots: the Vosges block the Atlantic rain, giving warm, sunny, dry autumns for slow aromatic ripening."],
["Crémant d'Alsace","Traditional-method sparkling, largely Pinot Blanc: the largest of France's Crémants, second only to Champagne among its sparkling appellations; its rosé must be all Pinot Noir."],
["Signature profiles","Riesling: lime, steel, stone dry. Gewurztraminer: lychee and rose, oily and low in acid. Pinot Gris: smoky orchard richness. Muscat: the one that smells of the grape itself."]],
traps:[
"Pinot Blanc is respected here but not noble: no Grand Cru.",
"Alsace Riesling is traditionally dry; German Kabinett often is not: the same grape, a different default.",
"Vendanges Tardives is late harvest and may finish nearly dry; Sélection de Grains Nobles is always sweet.",
"It is AOP Alsace, France: a German-looking bottle and German-sounding grapes under French law.",
"Pinot Noir is Alsace's only red, and since the 2022 vintage it can be Grand Cru: an older card that says no red may be is out of date."]},

{cat:"Rhône",
lead:"Two valleys under one name. The north: Syrah alone on steep granite terraces from Côte-Rôtie to Cornas, with Viognier at home in Condrieu. The south: Mediterranean blends led by Grenache, crowned by Châteauneuf-du-Pape and its thirteen permitted varieties.",
exam:"The northern crus and their grape rules (Viognier co-fermented in Côte-Rôtie, none in Cornas), Hermitage against Côte-Rôtie, Châteauneuf's grapes and stones, the southern ladder and its crus, Tavel, and the Vins Doux Naturels of Beaumes-de-Venise and Rasteau.",
facts:[
["Northern crus","Côte-Rôtie (Syrah with up to 20 per cent Viognier, co-fermented, on the Côte Brune and Côte Blonde), Condrieu and Château-Grillet (Viognier alone), Saint-Joseph, Crozes-Hermitage (the largest), Hermitage (the hill of power), Cornas (Syrah alone, no white grapes allowed) and Saint-Péray (still and sparkling white from Marsanne and Roussanne)."],
["Hermitage","Syrah for decades of ageing, and great whites from Marsanne and Roussanne. Jaboulet's 'La Chapelle', Chave and Chapoutier are the names."],
["Châteauneuf-du-Pape","Grenache-led blends with Syrah and Mourvèdre, many grown over galets roulés, round stones that store the day's heat. Thirteen varieties are allowed, eighteen if each colour form counts, whites among them. Its growers' rules of the 1920s were a model for France's AOC system. Many bottles are embossed with the papal keys. Rayas is all Grenache; Beaucastel uses all thirteen."],
["The southern ladder","Côtes du Rhône, then Côtes du Rhône Villages, then Villages with a named commune (Séguret, Sablet), then the crus: Châteauneuf-du-Pape, Gigondas, Vacqueyras, Vinsobres, Rasteau, Cairanne, Beaumes-de-Venise, Lirac, Tavel and Laudun, raised to cru for the 2024 vintage."],
["Tavel","Rosé only: Grenache-based, deep in colour, dry and made for the table. Lirac, beside it, makes all three colours."],
["Sweet and fortified","Muscat de Beaumes-de-Venise (a Vin Doux Naturel from Muscat) and Rasteau's Vin Doux Naturel (Grenache): mutage, a dose of grape spirit, stops the fermentation and keeps the sugar."],
["Climate and grapes","The north is continental, on steep slopes; the south is Mediterranean, dried and cleaned by the mistral, a cold north wind. Southern reds: Grenache, Syrah, Mourvèdre, Cinsault, Carignan. Whites: Viognier and Marsanne in the north, Grenache Blanc, Clairette and Bourboulenc in the south, Roussanne in both."],
["Structure signature","North: savoury, peppery, floral Syrah (the pepper is rotundone). South: raspberry, garrigue and warmth, with Grenache's alcohol."]],
traps:[
"Cornas is the all-Syrah cru; Côte-Rôtie may co-ferment Viognier: the pair is reversed on many a failed card.",
"Château-Grillet is a single-estate AOP inside Condrieu.",
"Crozes-Hermitage is the largest northern AOP, not a lesser Hermitage vineyard.",
"Tavel makes no red or white: rosé is the whole appellation.",
"Thirteen grapes and eighteen grapes describe the same Châteauneuf list: the larger count names each colour form separately."]},

{cat:"Southern France",
lead:"The Midi's arc from Provence to the Pyrenees: Bandol's Mourvèdre, the Languedoc's ocean of Rhône-style blends turning to quality (Pic Saint-Loup, Terrasses du Larzac), Roussillon's sweet Grenache, and the Southwest's stubborn originals: Cahors Malbec, Madiran Tannat and Jurançon's mountain sweet wines.",
exam:"Bandol and Mourvèdre, Provence rosé and its grapes, Cahors and Malbec (Côt), Madiran and Tannat, Jurançon's grapes and styles, Picpoul, Limoux, and the Vins Doux Naturels of Banyuls, Maury and Rivesaltes.",
facts:[
["Provence rosé","Pale and dry, from Grenache, Cinsault, Syrah and Mourvèdre. Côtes de Provence is the engine; Bandol's rosé is the age-worthy exception. Cassis is known for its white."],
["Bandol","Reds of at least 50 per cent Mourvèdre, often far more, with at least 18 months in wood: meaty, black-fruited, built to age. Domaine Tempier is the standard-bearer."],
["Languedoc","Syrah, Grenache, Mourvèdre and Carignan over garrigue. The risers: Pic Saint-Loup, Terrasses du Larzac, Faugères (schist), Saint-Chinian, La Clape, Minervois and Corbières. IGP Pays d'Oc is the varietal engine behind them."],
["Picpoul de Pinet","High-acid coastal white from Picpoul Blanc, the 'lip-stinger': the Midi's Muscadet, made for the oysters of the Étang de Thau."],
["Limoux","Blanquette de Limoux, chiefly Mauzac, claims a sparkling history older than Champagne's; Crémant de Limoux is led by Chardonnay and Chenin Blanc."],
["Cahors","Malbec, called Côt or Auxerrois here, at least 70 per cent: iron, leather and grip, drier and firmer than Mendoza's violet-scented style."],
["Madiran","Tannat: France's most tannic mainstream red. Micro-oxygenation was developed here to soften it. Cassoulet's match."],
["Jurançon","Pyrenean Gros and Petit Manseng, sec and moelleux; the sweet wines come from passerillage, grapes shrivelled on the vine, not from botrytis."],
["The wider Southwest","Bergerac beside Bordeaux, with sweet, botrytised Monbazillac; Gaillac and its local grapes, Mauzac, Len de l'El and Duras; Irouléguy in the Basque hills."],
["Roussillon","Banyuls and Maury: fortified sweet reds from Grenache, the classic wine for chocolate; Collioure is the dry wine of Banyuls's own hills. Rivesaltes is mostly Grenache too; Muscat de Rivesaltes is the Muscat."]],
traps:[
"Jurançon's sweetness comes from grapes shrivelled on the vine (passerillage), not noble rot.",
"Cahors Malbec reads rustic and ferrous: do not describe it like Argentine Malbec.",
"Bandol rosé exists and ages, but the exam's Bandol answer is Mourvèdre red.",
"Fitou, the Languedoc's oldest red AOP, lies inside Corbières country: Carignan territory.",
"Rivesaltes is not the Muscat: Muscat de Rivesaltes is."]},

{cat:"Jura, Savoie & Corsica",
lead:"The mountain and island collector's shelf: Jura's Vin Jaune aged under a veil of yeast, Savoie's alpine whites, and Corsica's Italian-accented Nielluccio and Vermentino. Small regions with an outsized place on the paper.",
exam:"Vin Jaune's grape, process and bottle; Vin de Paille; Savagnin against Sauvignon; Savoie's Jacquère, Altesse and Mondeuse; Corsica's grapes and Muscat du Cap Corse; Macvin.",
facts:[
["Vin Jaune","Savagnin aged for at least six years and three months, most of it in cask under a film of yeast (sous voile) with no topping up: nutty, curry-spiced, saline. Bottled in the 62 cl clavelin. Château-Chalon makes nothing else."],
["Vin de Paille","Jura's sweet wine from grapes dried on straw or racks for at least six weeks: the passito idea with a French address, sold in half bottles."],
["Jura reds and whites","Poulsard, Trousseau and Pinot Noir for red; Chardonnay and Savagnin made either fresh and topped up ('ouillé') or oxidative. Arbois was among France's first AOCs, in 1936. Crémant du Jura is serious value."],
["Savoie","Jacquère (Apremont, Abymes): featherweight alpine white. Altesse, also called Roussette: richer. Chignin-Bergeron is Roussanne. Mondeuse: peppery alpine red."],
["Corsica","Nielluccio (genetically Sangiovese) leads in Patrimonio; Sciaccarello, pale and peppery, in Ajaccio; Vermentino for the whites; Muscat du Cap Corse is a Vin Doux Naturel."],
["Macvin du Jura","Grape must stopped with Jura marc before it ferments: a mistelle, cousin to Pineau des Charentes."]],
traps:[
"Savagnin is Jura's grape; Sauvignon is a different word and a different wine.",
"Vin Jaune is never fortified: a Sherry-like flavour at table-wine strength.",
"The clavelin is 62 cl, said to be what remains of a litre after the years in cask.",
"Nielluccio is Sangiovese in Corsican dress: Tuscany's grape on French soil."]},

{cat:"Italy North",
lead:"From Piedmont's autumn fog to the Veneto's drying lofts: Nebbiolo's tar-and-roses kingdom in Barolo and Barbaresco, Amarone's dried-grape concentration, Prosecco's tank-method ocean, and the alpine whites of Alto Adige and Friuli.",
exam:"Barolo and Barbaresco ageing and character, Nebbiolo's other names, Amarone and Ripasso, Prosecco's grape, method and DOCGs, Soave and Gavi, and the traditional method of Franciacorta and Trento.",
facts:[
["Barolo and Barbaresco","All Nebbiolo. Barolo: 38 months' ageing with 18 in wood, Riserva 62. Barbaresco: 26 months with 9 in wood, Riserva 50. Both name their crus as MGAs (menzioni geografiche aggiuntive). Tar and roses, high acid and high tannin with a pale garnet colour: the great deceiver."],
["Nebbiolo elsewhere","Spanna in Gattinara and Ghemme, Chiavennasca in Valtellina, and Roero and Langhe Nebbiolo nearer home: the same grape in other places."],
["Piedmont's everyday wines","Barbera (high acid, low tannin, the people's red), Dolcetto (soft, drunk young), Moscato d'Asti (gently sparkling, about 5 per cent alcohol), Gavi from Cortese, Arneis in Roero."],
["Amarone della Valpolicella","A Corvina-led blend with Rondinella; the grapes dry for months in lofts (appassimento) and then ferment dry to at least 14 per cent alcohol, often more. Recioto della Valpolicella is the sweet original; Ripasso is Valpolicella refermented on Amarone or Recioto skins."],
["Prosecco","Glera, made by the tank (Charmat) method for freshness. Conegliano Valdobbiadene and Asolo are the DOCGs, with Cartizze the most prized hill; the DOC below spreads across Veneto and Friuli. Prosecco Rosé, Glera with 10 to 15 per cent Pinot Nero, joined the DOC in 2020."],
["Franciacorta","Lombardy's traditional-method sparkling: Chardonnay, Pinot Nero and Pinot Bianco, with at least 18 months on the lees for the non-vintage. Satèn is its softer style: white grapes only, Brut, at lower pressure."],
["Trentino-Alto Adige","Trento DOC is Italy's other great traditional-method sparkling. Alto Adige's reds are Lagrein and Schiava; Teroldego is Trentino's."],
["Soave and the northeast","Garganega on Soave Classico's volcanic hills. Friuli and Alto Adige make Italy's precision whites: Pinot Grigio with real character, Friulano, Ribolla Gialla."]],
traps:[
"Nebbiolo looks pale and smells of flowers, then hits with heavy tannin: never call it light-bodied.",
"Prosecco is tank method; Franciacorta and Trento are traditional method: never swap them.",
"Amarone is dry; Recioto della Valpolicella is the sweet one.",
"Gavi and Soave are places: Gavi's grape is Cortese, Soave's is Garganega."]},

{cat:"Italy Central & South",
lead:"Sangiovese's Tuscan heartland (Chianti Classico, Brunello, Vino Nobile), then south past Montepulciano d'Abruzzo's confusing name to Campania's Aglianico, Puglia's Primitivo, the islands, and the volcanic revival on Etna.",
exam:"Chianti Classico's rules and black rooster, Brunello's grape and ageing, the Montepulciano grape-and-town trap, the Super Tuscans, Aglianico's DOCGs, Primitivo and Zinfandel, Etna's Nerello, and the island grapes.",
facts:[
["Chianti Classico","At least 80 per cent Sangiovese from the historic zone between Florence and Siena, under the black rooster (Gallo Nero). Riserva: 24 months. Gran Selezione at the top: estate fruit, 30 months, at least 90 per cent Sangiovese with native grapes only from the 2027 vintage, and the right to name one of eleven village areas (UGAs) such as Radda or Panzano."],
["Chianti","A separate and larger DOCG around the Classico zone. Sangiovese: at least 70 per cent, the figure the Court prints and the rule for earlier vintages, lowered to 60 per cent from the 2026/27 harvest campaign by the rules amended in June 2026. Its subzones, such as Rufina and Colli Senesi, number eight since Terre di Vinci was added in 2026."],
["Brunello di Montalcino","All Sangiovese, called Brunello here and long known as Sangiovese Grosso, with two years in wood, released in the fifth year after the harvest, the sixth for Riserva. Biondi-Santi is credited with the first bottlings in the late nineteenth century. Rosso di Montalcino is the earlier-drinking sibling."],
["Vino Nobile di Montepulciano","Sangiovese, here called Prugnolo Gentile, from the TOWN of Montepulciano in Tuscany: the name is not the grape."],
["Montepulciano d'Abruzzo","The GRAPE Montepulciano in Abruzzo: dark, soft, everyday value. Its deep rosé, Cerasuolo d'Abruzzo, has its own DOC."],
["Super Tuscans","Wines first made outside the DOC rules, often with Bordeaux varieties. Sassicaia now has its own DOC, Bolgheri Sassicaia; Ornellaia is Bolgheri; Tignanello (Sangiovese-led) and Solaia sell as IGT Toscana."],
["Central whites and neighbours","Vernaccia di San Gimignano, Tuscany's white DOCG, was Italy's first DOC in 1966. Verdicchio comes from the Marche, Orvieto from Umbria, Frascati from near Rome. Sagrantino di Montefalco is Umbria's fiercely tannic red."],
["Campania","Aglianico in Taurasi DOCG, the 'Barolo of the South'; the whites Fiano di Avellino and Greco di Tufo, both DOCG: volcanic and age-worthy."],
["Puglia","Primitivo, the same grape as Zinfandel, in Manduria and Gioia del Colle; Negroamaro in Salice Salentino."],
["Sicily and Sardinia","Sicily: Nero d'Avola, Cerasuolo di Vittoria (Nero d'Avola with Frappato, the island's first DOCG) and Marsala. Sardinia: Cannonau, which is Grenache, and Vermentino di Gallura, the island's DOCG."],
["Etna","Nerello Mascalese on the volcano's slopes, the 'Burgundy of the Mediterranean'; Carricante for the whites; single-contrada bottlings name the crus."]],
traps:[
"Montepulciano is a Tuscan town (Sangiovese wine) AND an Abruzzo grape: a favourite Italian trap.",
"Brunello must be all Sangiovese; Chianti Classico needs only 80 per cent.",
"Primitivo and Zinfandel are one grape, and Croatia's Tribidrag (Crljenak Kaštelanski) is that same variety under its oldest name, not its parent.",
"Taurasi is Aglianico from Campania; Aglianico del Vulture is Basilicata's.",
"Chianti and Chianti Classico are separate DOCGs: only the Classico wears the black rooster."]},

{cat:"Spain",
lead:"Old vines, long oak and late releases: Rioja's Tempranillo aged to order (Crianza, Reserva, Gran Reserva), Ribera del Duero's darker take, Priorat's llicorella slate, Rías Baixas' Atlantic Albariño and Cava's traditional method, with Jerez waiting in the fortified chapter.",
exam:"The quality ladder (DO, DOCa, Vino de Pago), Rioja's zones and ageing tiers with their oak minimums, Tempranillo's other names, Priorat's soil and grapes, Albariño, Cava's grapes and method, and Rueda's Verdejo.",
facts:[
["The ladder","Vino de Pago (a single estate with its own appellation), DOCa (only Rioja and Priorat, which writes it DOQ in Catalan), DO, Vino de Calidad, then the regional Vino de la Tierra (IGP) and plain Vino."],
["Rioja","Three zones: Rioja Alta and Rioja Alavesa, cooled by the Atlantic, and the warmer Rioja Oriental (called Rioja Baja until 2018). Tempranillo leads, with Garnacha, Graciano and Mazuelo (Cariñena); Viura is the main white."],
["Rioja ageing (reds)","Crianza: two years, one in oak. Reserva: three years, one in oak. Gran Reserva: five years, two in oak and two in bottle. American oak's coconut and dill is the traditional signature."],
["Tempranillo's aliases","Tinto Fino or Tinta del País (Ribera del Duero), Tinta de Toro (Toro), Cencibel (La Mancha), Ull de Llebre (Catalonia), Tinta Roriz or Aragonez (Portugal)."],
["Ribera del Duero","High-altitude Tempranillo, darker and more tannic than Rioja. Vega Sicilia 'Único' is the legend, Pingus the cult."],
["Priorat","Garnacha and Cariñena on llicorella, crumbling slate: low yields, mineral power. Spain's second DOCa (DOQ), after Rioja. Montsant wraps around it."],
["Galicia","Rías Baixas: Albariño, saline, peachy and high in acid, from the Atlantic coast. Ribeira Sacra: Mencía on heroic river slopes. Valdeorras: Godello. Bierzo, next door in Castilla y León, is Mencía too."],
["Cava","Traditional method, mostly from Penedès in Catalonia, from Macabeo, Xarel·lo and Parellada. At least 9 months on the lees; Reserva 18, Gran Reserva 30, and the single-site Cava de Paraje Calificado 36."],
["Rueda and the rest","Verdejo in Rueda, herbal citrus close to Sauvignon Blanc's register; Tinta de Toro in Toro; Garnacha and rosado in Navarra; Monastrell (Mourvèdre) baking in Jumilla."]],
traps:[
"Gran Reserva needs five years with two in oak; Reserva three with one: candidates swap the numbers.",
"Priorat's DOQ status and its slate (llicorella) are both asked: name the rock.",
"Cava is traditional method, in bottle, not tank; its grapes are the Catalan trio, not Chardonnay first.",
"Mencía is the red of Bierzo and Ribeira Sacra: fresh and floral, nothing like Monastrell.",
"Rioja Oriental and Rioja Baja are one zone under two names: the new name dates from 2018."]},

{cat:"Portugal",
lead:"Beyond Port's lodges: the Douro's dry reds from the same steep schist, Vinho Verde's light Atlantic whites, the Dão's granite elegance, Bairrada's tannic Baga and the Alentejo's warm plains, all from the world's densest treasury of native grapes.",
exam:"The Douro's dry-wine grapes (Touriga Nacional and company), Vinho Verde's style and Alvarinho, the Dão and Bairrada signatures, the Alentejo, and the Port and Madeira basics that carry over from the fortified chapter.",
facts:[
["Douro","Three subregions upriver, Baixo Corgo, Cima Corgo and Douro Superior, on steep schist. Dry reds from the Port grapes, Touriga Nacional, Touriga Franca, Tinta Roriz (Tempranillo), Tinto Cão and Tinta Barroca; Barca Velha (Ferreira, first vintage 1952) proved what they could do."],
["Vinho Verde","'Green' means young, not the colour: light, low in alcohol, often with a faint prickle, and white, red or rosé. Alvarinho from Monção e Melgaço is the serious tier; Loureiro and Arinto are the other key whites."],
["Dão","A granite plateau sheltered by mountains and pine forest: elegant reds from Touriga Nacional, Jaen (Spain's Mencía) and Alfrocheiro, and Encruzado, one of Portugal's finest whites."],
["Bairrada","Baga on clay: high-acid, high-tannin reds that age like an Atlantic cousin of Nebbiolo; also much traditional-method sparkling."],
["Alentejo","Warm southern plains: generous blends of Aragonez, Trincadeira and Alicante Bouschet, a red-fleshed grape that thrives here. Wine fermented in clay talhas is an old tradition revived, now recognised as Vinho de Talha."],
["Setúbal and the coast","Moscatel de Setúbal: fortified Muscat with decades of stamina. Colares grows ungrafted Ramisco in sand by the sea. Lisboa and Tejo are the value coast."],
["The labels","DOC (also written DOP) is the top tier, Vinho Regional (IGP) the regional one, then plain Vinho."]],
traps:[
"Vinho Verde can be red, white or rosé: the name means young wine, not green grapes.",
"Tinta Roriz and Aragonez are both Tempranillo: one grape, two Portuguese names by region.",
"Alicante Bouschet is red-fleshed (a teinturier): one of the few grapes with coloured juice.",
"Encruzado belongs to the Dão: the white the exam expects you to place.",
"Douro DOC is the dry wine; Porto is the fortified appellation from the same vineyards."]},

{cat:"Germany",
lead:"Riesling at the climatic edge: slate rivers, featherweight Mosel Kabinetts, and a Prädikat ladder that ranks by ripeness at harvest, not sweetness in the glass. The VDP lays a Burgundian vineyard pyramid over it, with Grosses Gewächs as its dry Grand Cru.",
exam:"The Prädikat ladder in order, trocken and feinherb, the regions and their signatures (Mosel, Rheingau, Pfalz, Franken), the VDP and GG, Spätburgunder, and how Eiswein is made.",
facts:[
["Prädikat ladder","Kabinett, Spätlese, Auslese, Beerenauslese (BA) and Trockenbeerenauslese (TBA), with Eiswein at BA's must weight: each step a higher minimum ripeness at picking, measured as must weight (Oechsle). Below them: Qualitätswein, Landwein and plain Deutscher Wein."],
["Dry vocabulary","Trocken means dry; halbtrocken and the looser feinherb mean off-dry. A Spätlese trocken is a RIPE dry wine: the Prädikat measures sugar at picking, not what is left in the glass."],
["The regions","Thirteen: Mosel, Rheingau, Rheinhessen (the largest), Pfalz, Nahe, Franken, Baden, Württemberg, Ahr, Mittelrhein, Hessische Bergstrasse, and Saale-Unstrut and Sachsen in the east."],
["Mosel","Blue and grey slate on giddy slopes (Bernkastel, Wehlen, Piesport), with the Saar and Ruwer: featherweight, electric Riesling, often 7.5 to 9 per cent alcohol when sweetness balances the acid."],
["Rheingau","Where the Rhine runs west, south-facing slopes give Riesling with more body and a long history; Schloss Johannisberg claims the Spätlese legend of 1775. Assmannshausen is its Spätburgunder village."],
["Pfalz and Baden","Warmer and drier, leaning to dry wines. Baden is Spätburgunder (Pinot Noir) country, and Germany is among the world's biggest growers of Pinot Noir."],
["Franken","Silvaner and Riesling, often bone dry, in the flat, round Bocksbeutel flask."],
["VDP pyramid","Gutswein, Ortswein, Erste Lage, Grosse Lage. A dry wine from a Grosse Lage is Grosses Gewächs (GG). The eagle on the capsule marks a VDP member estate; GG bottles carry the letters GG."],
["Eiswein","Healthy grapes frozen ON the vine at minus 7°C or colder and pressed while frozen, so the water stays behind as ice: concentration without botrytis."]],
traps:[
"Auslese can be fermented dry and Kabinett can taste sweet: the Prädikat is not the sweetness in the glass.",
"Eiswein needs BA must weight but comes from frost, not noble rot.",
"Liebfraumilch and the bulk of Rheinhessen are history's cautionary tale, not its rule: Rheinhessen now holds top growers (Keller, Wittmann).",
"Spätburgunder is Pinot Noir; Grauburgunder is Pinot Gris; Weissburgunder is Pinot Blanc.",
"The eagle on the capsule says VDP member, not Grand Cru."]},

{cat:"Austria",
lead:"Grüner Veltliner's white-pepper empire along the Danube (Wachau, Kremstal, Kamptal), Riesling on terraces of ancient rock, Blaufränkisch in Burgenland, Sauvignon Blanc in Styria, and a DAC system that ties each region to its signature grapes.",
exam:"Grüner Veltliner's profile and homes, the Wachau's own ladder (Steinfeder, Federspiel, Smaragd), the DACs and their tiers, Blaufränkisch and Zweigelt, Styrian Sauvignon Blanc, and Ruster Ausbruch.",
facts:[
["Grüner Veltliner","White pepper, lentil and radish over citrus and orchard fruit; from light, spritzy litre-bottle Heuriger wine to age-worthy Smaragd. Austria's flag and its most planted grape."],
["Wachau ladder","Steinfeder (lightest), Federspiel, Smaragd (richest, named for an emerald lizard): the Vinea Wachau's own terms, not national law. Since 2020 the Wachau has also been a DAC."],
["Danube trio","Wachau, Kremstal and Kamptal: Grüner and Riesling on gneiss and loess terraces, dry, precise and mineral."],
["DAC system","Districtus Austriae Controllatus: each DAC ties a region to its typical grapes, Weinviertel DAC to Grüner, Mittelburgenland DAC to Blaufränkisch. Within a DAC, labels climb from regional wine (Gebietswein) to village wine (Ortswein) to single-vineyard wine (Riedenwein)."],
["Burgenland reds","Blaufränkisch: dark cherry, pepper and structure. Zweigelt, a crossing of Blaufränkisch and St Laurent: softer and juicy. Both grow around the shallow Neusiedlersee."],
["Styria and Vienna","Steiermark: Sauvignon Blanc of real class on steep southern hills (Südsteiermark DAC), and Schilcher, a sharp rosé from Blauer Wildbacher. Vienna: Wiener Gemischter Satz, a field blend with its own DAC."],
["Sweet Austria","The Neusiedlersee's autumn mists breed botrytis. Ruster Ausbruch, once a rung between BA and TBA, is now a DAC of its own, Rust only and picked at TBA must weight; Eiswein and Schilfwein, from grapes dried on reed mats, besides."]],
traps:[
"Smaragd, Federspiel and Steinfeder belong ONLY to the Wachau and its growers' association, not to Austrian law.",
"Zweigelt is a twentieth-century crossing and Austria's most planted red, not an ancient grape.",
"Grüner's tell is white pepper and lentil, not perfume like Muscat: a different kind of spice.",
"Austrian Riesling is dry by default: do not carry German sweetness habits across the border."]},

{cat:"Hungary & Greece",
lead:"Two ancient wine nations: Tokaji's botrytised aszú berries, once counted in puttonyos, dry Furmint's rise and Eger's Bull's Blood; then Greece's Assyrtiko in Santorini's basket-trained vines, Naoussa's Xinomavro and Nemea's Agiorgitiko.",
exam:"Tokaji Aszú's process and sweetness rules, Furmint, Eszencia and Szamorodni, Egri Bikavér; Assyrtiko and Santorini, Xinomavro against Nebbiolo, Agiorgitiko, Moschofilero and retsina.",
facts:[
["Tokaji Aszú","Botrytised (aszú) berries soaked in base wine or must. Sweetness was counted in puttonyos; today Aszú needs at least 120 g/L of sugar, the puttonyos number is optional, and only 5 and 6 survive. Szamorodni, 'as it comes', is made from whole bunches, dry or sweet."],
["Eszencia","The free-run juice of aszú berries: at least 450 g/L of sugar, so sweet it barely ferments, often to only a few per cent alcohol. Served by the spoon."],
["Hungary's grapes","Furmint (fiery acid, now superb dry), Hárslevelű and Sárgamuskotály in Tokaj; Kékfrankos (Blaufränkisch) is the largest share of Egri Bikavér, Eger's 'Bull's Blood' blend."],
["Santorini","Assyrtiko on ash and pumice with almost no rain, the vines coiled into kouloura baskets against the wind and never grafted: saline, searing, ageable. Vinsanto, from sun-dried Assyrtiko and Aidani, is the island's sweet, amber-to-mahogany wine."],
["Naoussa","Xinomavro, 'acid-black': pale colour, a nose of tomato, olive and dried flowers, ferocious acid and tannin. Greece's answer to Nebbiolo."],
["Nemea","Agiorgitiko (St George): a plush, spicy red from the Peloponnese, and one of Greece's most planted reds."],
["Mantinia","Moschofilero, a pink-skinned grape, makes a floral, crisp white on the Peloponnese's high plateau."],
["Retsina and modern Greece","Retsina, white wine flavoured with Aleppo pine resin, is the historical footnote; the modern story is Assyrtiko, Xinomavro and Malagousia's revival."]],
traps:[
"Puttonyos 3 and 4 were abolished: modern Aszú starts at the old 5 puttonyos level.",
"Kékfrankos is Blaufränkisch: Hungary and Austria share the grape.",
"Xinomavro mimics Nebbiolo in every structural way: a classic blind-tasting decoy pair.",
"Vinsanto (Santorini) and Vin Santo (Tuscany) are different wines with near-identical names."]},

{cat:"Producers & Icons",
lead:"The names that anchor wine lists and auctions: DRC and d'Yquem, Gaja and Sassicaia, Vega Sicilia and Penfolds Grange. The exam joins producer, region and bottling in one breath: flashcard territory, and the Codex's own Producers view holds the full roster.",
exam:"Match producer to region to flagship: DRC and Romanée-Conti, Guigal's 'La La' Côte-Rôties, Biondi-Santi and Brunello, Château d'Yquem and Sauternes, Egon Müller and the Scharzhofberger, Penfolds and Grange, Catena and Mendoza.",
facts:[
["Burgundy","Domaine de la Romanée-Conti (the monopoles Romanée-Conti and La Tâche), Leroy, Rousseau (Chambertin), Leflaive and Coche-Dury for whites, Raveneau in Chablis."],
["Bordeaux","The five First Growths; Pétrus and Le Pin (Pomerol); Cheval Blanc and Ausone (Saint-Émilion); d'Yquem alone as Premier Cru Supérieur."],
["Champagne","Krug (the multi-vintage Grande Cuvée), Salon (Chardonnay from Le Mesnil-sur-Oger, made only in chosen years), Bollinger (Pinot-led, partly barrel-fermented), Louis Roederer's Cristal, Moët's Dom Pérignon."],
["Rhône","Guigal's single-vineyard Côte-Rôties (La Mouline, La Landonne, La Turque), Chave in Hermitage, Jaboulet's 'La Chapelle', Beaucastel and Rayas in Châteauneuf-du-Pape."],
["Loire and Alsace","Huet (Vouvray), Clos Rougeard (Saumur-Champigny), Coulée de Serrant (a single-estate appellation within Savennières), Didier Dagueneau (Pouilly-Fumé); Trimbach's Clos Sainte Hune and Zind-Humbrecht in Alsace."],
["Italy","Gaja (Barbaresco), Giacomo Conterno's 'Monfortino' (Barolo), Biondi-Santi (the first Brunello), Sassicaia (Bolgheri), Quintarelli and Dal Forno (Amarone)."],
["Iberia","Vega Sicilia 'Único', Pingus, López de Heredia's Viña Tondonia (Rioja's time capsule), Álvaro Palacios's 'L'Ermita' (Priorat), Niepoort and Taylor's in Port."],
["Germany and Austria","Egon Müller (the Scharzhofberger, whose TBA is among the world's most expensive whites), JJ Prüm (Wehlener Sonnenuhr), Dönnhoff, Keller; FX Pichler and Knoll in the Wachau."],
["New World","Screaming Eagle and Harlan (Napa), Ridge Monte Bello, Penfolds Grange (multi-region Shiraz), Henschke Hill of Grace (one vineyard), Catena Zapata (Mendoza altitude), Sadie Family's Columella (Swartland)."]],
traps:[
"Grange is a multi-vineyard, multi-region blend; Hill of Grace is one vineyard: the classic Australian contrast.",
"Romanée-Conti and La Tâche are DRC monopoles; Richebourg and Romanée-Saint-Vivant are shared with other owners.",
"López de Heredia releases late on purpose, after long ageing: the oxidative style is intent, not fault.",
"Egon Müller's fame is sweet Riesling from the Saar, not dry GG.",
"Pétrus and Le Pin are Pomerol; Cheval Blanc and Ausone are Saint-Émilion: the Right Bank pairs candidates swap."]},

{cat:"California",
lead:"America's engine room: Napa Cabernet from valley-floor, bench and mountain AVAs, Sonoma's cool Pinot Noir and Chardonnay, old-vine Zinfandel and the Central Coast's rise, all under the AVA system, which draws lines on the map but sets no rules for grapes.",
exam:"AVA geography (Napa's sub-AVAs, Russian River Valley, Sta. Rita Hills), the labelling percentages, Zinfandel's identity, the Judgment of Paris, Prohibition, and the key producers.",
facts:[
["Labelling law","An AVA on the label: at least 85 per cent of the grapes from it. A grape named: at least 75 per cent of that variety. A county: 75 per cent from it. 'California': every grape from the state. A vintage: 95 per cent from that year with an AVA, 85 per cent otherwise."],
["Napa's map","Valley-floor and bench AVAs: Oakville, Rutherford ('Rutherford dust'), Stags Leap District (home of the 1976 winner), St. Helena and Calistoga, with cool Los Carneros at the southern end, shared with Sonoma. Mountain AVAs: Howell Mountain, Diamond Mountain District, Spring Mountain District, Mount Veeder, Atlas Peak. A wine from a Napa sub-AVA must also name Napa Valley on its label."],
["Sonoma","Russian River Valley: fog-cooled Pinot Noir and Chardonnay. Alexander Valley: Cabernet. Dry Creek Valley: Zinfandel. Sonoma Coast: the cold, saline frontier."],
["Judgment of Paris, 1976","Stag's Leap Wine Cellars' 1973 Cabernet and Chateau Montelena's 1973 Chardonnay beat First Growths and white Burgundies, tasted blind in Paris: California's founding legend and a favourite question."],
["History","Prohibition (1920 to 1933) closed most wineries. The modern era dates from the 1960s, with Robert Mondavi's Oakville winery of 1966 as its emblem."],
["Zinfandel","Genetically Primitivo and Croatia's Tribidrag: brambly, jammy, high in alcohol, from ancient vines in Lodi, Dry Creek and Amador. White Zinfandel, the off-dry pink born of a stuck fermentation, is credited with saving the old vines."],
["Central Coast","Sta. Rita Hills and Santa Maria Valley, where valleys running east to west funnel in the fog, for Pinot Noir and Chardonnay; Paso Robles (the Rhône Rangers, and Cabernet); Monterey; the Santa Cruz Mountains, home of Ridge Monte Bello."],
["Mendocino and the north","Anderson Valley: Alsace varieties and sparkling wine (Roederer Estate), the deep-fog Pinot Noir frontier."]],
traps:[
"An AVA guarantees geography only: no rules on grape, yield or style, unlike an AOP.",
"The federal varietal minimum is 75 per cent: a 'Cabernet' may legally hide a quarter of something else.",
"Rutherford and Oakville are valley-floor prestige; Howell Mountain fruit is a different, grippier animal: know which is which.",
"Chateau Montelena won Paris with CHARDONNAY; the red winner was Stag's Leap Wine Cellars.",
"Stag's Leap Wine Cellars and Stags' Leap Winery are different estates, and the AVA, Stags Leap District, has no apostrophe at all."]},

{cat:"Pacific NW, NY & Canada",
lead:"Oregon's Willamette Valley Pinot Noir, Washington's irrigated desert Cabernet and Syrah in the rain shadow of the Cascades, New York's Finger Lakes Riesling beside deep glacial lakes, and Canada's Icewine in Niagara.",
exam:"Willamette's sub-AVAs and soils, Oregon's stricter labelling, Columbia Valley geography (it is not rainy), Walla Walla, Finger Lakes Riesling, and the VQA Icewine rules.",
facts:[
["Willamette Valley","Cool and Burgundian, Pinot Noir first. Soils: volcanic Jory in the Dundee Hills, marine sediment in Yamhill-Carlton and Ribbon Ridge, wind-blown loess in the Chehalem Mountains; Eola-Amity Hills and McMinnville are among the other sub-AVAs. David Lett of The Eyrie Vineyards planted the valley's first Pinot Noir in 1965 and made his vineyard in the Dundee Hills."],
["Oregon law","Stricter than federal: a grape named needs 90 per cent of it, except a list of commonly blended varieties, Cabernet Sauvignon and Syrah among them, held at the federal 75; an AVA named needs 95 per cent of the fruit from it."],
["Washington","Nearly all vineyards lie EAST of the Cascades in irrigated shrub-steppe, on sandy soils left by the Ice Age Missoula Floods. The Columbia Valley spans Yakima Valley, Red Mountain (hot and tannic), the Walla Walla Valley and Horse Heaven Hills. Many vines grow on their own roots, since phylloxera finds the sand and the cold winters hard going."],
["Washington's wines","Cabernet Sauvignon, Merlot and Syrah (the Rocks District's savoury funk), and Riesling made at scale, Chateau Ste. Michelle its best-known name."],
["New York","Finger Lakes: deep glacial lakes temper brutal winters for dry and off-dry Riesling of real class. Dr Konstantin Frank pioneered vinifera on Keuka Lake in 1962; Seneca and Cayuga are the other key lakes. Long Island's North Fork: maritime Merlot and Cabernet Franc."],
["Canada","VQA Icewine: grapes frozen on the vine and picked at minus 8°C or colder, pressed while frozen; Vidal and Riesling, Inniskillin the icon. The Niagara Peninsula, between Lakes Ontario and Erie, leads Ontario. British Columbia's Okanagan Valley: desert-lake reds and aromatic whites."]],
traps:[
"Seattle's rain is a coastal story: the Columbia Valley is a desert that needs irrigation.",
"Oregon's 90 per cent varietal rule beats the federal 75: a frequent 'which is stricter' question.",
"Canada's minus 8°C for Icewine is law; artificial freezing (cryoextraction) is excluded.",
"The Rocks District of Milton-Freewater lies wholly in Oregon, yet it is a sub-AVA of the Walla Walla Valley, an AVA shared with Washington: geography's little joke."]},

{cat:"South America",
lead:"Altitude and the Andes: Mendoza Malbec from Luján de Cuyo and the high Uco Valley, Salta's floral Torrontés in some of the world's highest vineyards, Chile's ribbon of valleys running from a cold coast to the cordillera with Carmenère as its signature, and rising corners in Uruguay (Tannat) and Brazil (sparkling).",
exam:"Malbec's Mendoza homes and what altitude does, Torrontés and Salta's extremes, Bonarda's true identity, Carmenère's history and profile, Chile's regions and valley signatures, Uruguay's Tannat.",
facts:[
["Mendoza Malbec","Malbec is the Côt of Cahors, made Argentina's own. Luján de Cuyo, the classic plush heartland and a DOC, lies at roughly 900 to 1,100 m; the Uco Valley (Tupungato, Gualtallary, Altamira) climbs to 1,500 m and beyond on stony, often calcareous benches. Altitude trades heat for sun: deep colour, violet perfume and fresh acid."],
["Salta & Torrontés","In Salta's Calchaquí Valleys, Cafayate sits at about 1,700 m and vineyards further up the valley pass 3,000 m, among the highest in the world. Torrontés Riojano, a cross of Muscat of Alexandria and Criolla Chica (Listán Prieto), gives a jasmine and peach nose over a dry palate."],
["Argentina beyond Mendoza","Bonarda, the country's second red, is the Douce Noire of Savoie (California's Charbono), not an Italian Bonarda. San Juan grows Syrah in hot valleys; Patagonia's Río Negro and Neuquén give cooler Pinot Noir and Malbec. The law nests IP and IG names, with two DOCs, Luján de Cuyo and San Rafael."],
["Carmenère","An old Bordeaux variety, sold in Chile as Merlot until 1994, when ampelography unmasked it (DNA confirmed it later). It ripens weeks after Merlot, and picked early it shows a green pyrazine edge over black fruit; Colchagua (Apalta) and Cachapoal (Peumo) give it the long warm season it needs."],
["Chile's geography","Transverse valleys run from the Andes to the Pacific, and since 2011 a label may add Costa, Entre Cordilleras or Andes for the band the fruit grew in. North to south: Coquimbo (Limarí's Chardonnay on limestone and fog, Elqui's high Syrah), Aconcagua (with coastal Casablanca and San Antonio's Leyda for Sauvignon Blanc and Pinot Noir), the Central Valley (Maipo, Rapel with Cachapoal and Colchagua, Curicó, Maule), then the cooler, wetter South (Itata, Bío-Bío, Malleco)."],
["Chile's signatures","Maipo is Cabernet's home, the 'Bordeaux of Chile', with Puente Alto on the gravels of Alto Maipo; Colchagua grows Carmenère and Syrah; Maule's dry-farmed old Carignan (the VIGNO group) and Itata's old País and Cinsault lead the old-vine revival. Variety, vintage and valley each need 75%, or 85% for wine bound for the European Union."],
["Phylloxera footnote","Chile has never had phylloxera: desert to the north, the Andes to the east, the Pacific to the west and ice to the south fence it in, so vinifera stands on its own roots."],
["Uruguay & Brazil","Uruguay's national grape is Tannat, brought by Basque settlers in the 1870s and softened by a humid Atlantic climate, grown mostly in Canelones near Montevideo. Brazil's Serra Gaúcha, home of the Vale dos Vinhedos, is best known for sparkling wine."]],
traps:[
"Carmenère passed as Merlot in Chile until 1994; the date and the mistaken identity are both asked.",
"Torrontés smells sweet, from its Muscat parent, but usually finishes dry: a classic blind-tasting feint.",
"The Uco Valley sits higher and cooler than Luján de Cuyo: the style turns floral and taut, not bigger.",
"Casablanca is coastal and cool, a white-wine valley: do not file it with warm Colchagua.",
"Argentine Bonarda is Douce Noire, California's Charbono; the Bonarda of Oltrepò Pavese is Croatina, another grape again.",
"Apalta is in Colchagua and Peumo in Cachapoal: both sit inside the Rapel Valley."]},

{cat:"Australia",
lead:"Not one hot country but a federation of climates: Barossa's ancient own-rooted Shiraz, McLaren Vale's Grenache by the sea, Coonawarra Cabernet on terra rossa, the screwcapped dry Riesling of Clare and Eden Valley, Margaret River's Bordeaux-like poise, the Hunter's ageless Semillon, and cool Tasmania rising.",
exam:"Regional signatures (Barossa against Coonawarra against Clare), terra rossa, the 85% labelling rule and the GI ladder, GSM blends, Hunter Semillon's strange ageing, Penfolds Grange against Henschke's Hill of Grace.",
facts:[
["Barossa Valley","Warm-climate Shiraz, opulent and chocolatey. South Australia has kept phylloxera out, so vines planted in the 1840s still stand on their own roots, and the Barossa Old Vine Charter grades them from Old Vine (35 years) to Ancestor (125 years and more). Eden Valley, in the hills alongside and part of the Barossa zone, is Riesling country."],
["McLaren Vale & GSM","Maritime and Mediterranean, south of Adelaide: old-vine Grenache and Shiraz. GSM (Grenache, Shiraz and Mourvèdre, the last often sold as Mataro) is the southern Rhône blend Australia made its own, here and in the Barossa."],
["Clare & Eden Riesling","Bone-dry, lime and slate, ageing to toast and kerosene over a decade. Clare's Riesling makers moved to screwcap together with the 2000 vintage and set off the closure revolution; Grosset Polish Hill is the touchstone."],
["Coonawarra","A narrow strip of terra rossa (red loam over limestone) on the Limestone Coast, far south of Adelaide: cool for Australia, it gives structured Cabernet of cassis, eucalyptus and mint; Wynns is the benchmark."],
["Margaret River","Maritime Western Australia, planted from 1967: Cabernet and Cabernet-Merlot of near-Bordeaux poise, Chardonnay of world standing (Leeuwin's Art Series) and crisp Semillon-Sauvignon Blanc blends."],
["Hunter Valley Semillon","New South Wales, warm and humid: Semillon picked early at about 10 to 11% alcohol, neutral and lemony when young, then aged in bottle into toast, honey and lanolin with no oak at all, one of wine's strangest transformations."],
["Victoria & Tasmania","Yarra Valley (Pinot Noir, Chardonnay, restrained Shiraz), Mornington Peninsula, and Heathcote and Grampians for Shiraz; Tasmania, the coolest state, for sparkling base and Pinot Noir; Rutherglen bakes its Muscat (see Fortified Wines)."],
["Labels & law","85% for a stated variety, region or vintage, and blends name their grapes in descending order. The GI system (zone, region, subregion) draws borders only and sets no rules on grape or yield. Bin numbers (Penfolds Bin 389) are house codes, not law."]],
traps:[
"Hill of Grace is one Eden Valley vineyard (Henschke); Grange is a multi-regional South Australian blend (Penfolds). The pairing is exam gold.",
"Hunter Semillon is picked low in alcohol and never sees oak: its toastiness is bottle age alone.",
"Coonawarra is cool for Australia: its Cabernet is structured, not jammy.",
"Australian Riesling defaults to dry, the Clare and Eden style, unlike a German expectation.",
"Old Barossa vines are not 'pre-phylloxera' survivors: phylloxera never reached South Australia, so they never needed grafting."]},

{cat:"New Zealand",
lead:"Among the world's most maritime vineyards, none of them far from the sea: Marlborough Sauvignon Blanc's gooseberry and passionfruit built modern New Zealand in a generation, while Central Otago's Pinot Noir, among the world's southernmost, and Hawke's Bay's Gimblett Gravels add depth.",
exam:"Marlborough's style and subregions, Central Otago's semi-continental exception, Hawke's Bay Syrah and Bordeaux blends, Martinborough Pinot Noir, the map from Gisborne to Waipara, the screwcap.",
facts:[
["Marlborough","The Wairau, the Southern Valleys and the Awatere: pungent Sauvignon Blanc of gooseberry, passionfruit and green pepper, mostly machine-harvested, cool-fermented in steel and screwcapped. Montana planted here in 1973, and Cloudy Bay's first vintage, 1985, lit the fuse abroad; Pinot Noir and sparkling grow here too."],
["Central Otago","Schist, loess and a semi-continental climate at about 45°S, among the southernmost wine regions on earth: Pinot Noir of dark cherry and wild thyme. Subregions include Gibbston, Bannockburn, Bendigo, Cromwell and Wanaka."],
["Hawke's Bay","Warm North Island country: the Gimblett Gravels' stony heat ripens peppery, Rhône-like Syrah and Merlot-Cabernet blends, with fine Chardonnay alongside."],
["Martinborough & Wairarapa","At the foot of the North Island: small, wind-pruned Pinot Noir of savoury depth; Ata Rangi is the name to know."],
["The rest of the map","Gisborne for Chardonnay; Auckland and Waiheke Island for boutique reds; Nelson for aromatics and Pinot Noir; North Canterbury, with the Waipara Valley, for Riesling and Pinot Noir."],
["Whites beyond Sauvignon","Aromatic Pinot Gris and Riesling in the south; Chardonnay (Kumeu River) quietly world-class."],
["Screwcap & labels","The New Zealand Screwcap Wine Seal Initiative began in 2001, and screwcap now seals the great majority of the country's wine; New Zealand and Australia led the closure revolution. Labels follow an 85% rule for variety, vintage and region."]],
traps:[
"Central Otago is semi-continental: the exception in an otherwise maritime country.",
"Awatere (cooler, higher, herbal and saline) against Wairau (riper, tropical): Marlborough has its own internal geography.",
"New Zealand Syrah is cool-climate and peppery, closer to Crozes-Hermitage than to the Barossa.",
"Passionfruit and boxwood come from thiols, green pepper and cut grass from methoxypyrazines: know which compound family gives which note.",
"Central Otago is often called the world's southernmost wine region, but small Patagonian plantings lie further south: say 'among the southernmost'."]},

{cat:"South Africa",
lead:"Wine since 1659, reborn after 1994: the world's largest planting of Chenin Blanc, Stellenbosch Cabernet, the Swartland's old-vine revolution, Pinotage's divisive identity, cool-coast Pinot Noir, and Constantia's resurrected sweet legend.",
exam:"Chenin (Steen) and its old vines, Pinotage's parentage, Stellenbosch against Swartland, Constantia's Vin de Constance, Walker Bay Pinot Noir, the Wine of Origin ladder, Cap Classique.",
facts:[
["Chenin Blanc / Steen","The Cape grows more Chenin than France does: once brandy and bulk fruit, now old-vine wine of honeyed depth (Sadie's Skurfberg, Alheit). The Old Vine Project's seal certifies vineyards of 35 years and more, with the planting date on the bottle."],
["Pinotage","Crossed in 1925 at Stellenbosch University from Pinot Noir and Cinsaut, then known locally as Hermitage: smoky and plummy, with a banana ester when fermented hot and fast, and a common part of a 'Cape Blend'."],
["Stellenbosch","The Cape's heartland for Cabernet and Bordeaux blends: granite and sandstone mountain slopes cooled by False Bay, and a university town; Kanonkop is a classic name."],
["Swartland","Dry-farmed old bush vines on shale, schist and granite: Chenin, Syrah and Mediterranean blends. The Sadie Family's Columella and Palladius wrote the region's manifesto around the turn of the century."],
["Cool corners","Walker Bay's Hemel-en-Aarde (Hamilton Russell's Pinot Noir and Chardonnay), Elgin's apple-country whites, Cape Agulhas Sauvignon Blanc."],
["Constantia","Constantia's sweet Muscat de Frontignan was a legend of the 18th and 19th centuries, shipped to Napoleon in exile on St Helena; Klein Constantia revived it as Vin de Constance from 1986: unfortified, from grapes shrivelled on the vine."],
["WO system","Wine of Origin: 100% from the area named, 85% for a stated variety or vintage. The ladder runs geographical unit (Western Cape), region (Coastal Region, Breede River Valley), district (Stellenbosch, Swartland), ward (Constantia, Simonsberg-Stellenbosch). A seal on the neck certifies the claims and, since 2010, sustainable production; Cap Classique is the Cape's name for traditional-method sparkling wine."]],
traps:[
"Pinotage's parents are Pinot Noir and Cinsaut; 'Hermitage' was Cinsaut's local alias, hence the name.",
"Vin de Constance is not fortified: sun-shrivelled Muscat, unlike the fortified sweet wines of its day.",
"South Africa, not the Loire, is Chenin's largest home: a stock surprise fact.",
"Hemel-en-Aarde means Heaven and Earth: Walker Bay's Pinot Noir valley, not a brand."]},

{cat:"Sparkling Wine World",
lead:"Every bubble has a method: traditional, a second fermentation in the very bottle you buy, for Champagne, Cava, Franciacorta, Crémant and the serious New World; the tank (Charmat) method for Prosecco's fresh fruit; the ancestral method's single unfinished fermentation for pét-nat; and plain carbonation for the cheapest bottles.",
exam:"Method to wine, the lees minimums (Champagne 15 months, Cava 9, Franciacorta 18), the eight Crémants, Prosecco and Asti, the sweetness words, the pressure terms, English sparkling's chalk.",
facts:[
["Traditional method","Base wine plus liqueur de tirage referments in the bottle that is sold, then comes lees ageing, riddling, disgorging and dosage. Champagne, Cava, Franciacorta, the eight Crémants, Trento DOC (Ferrari), England, Cap Classique and most serious New World fizz."],
["Tank / Charmat","The second fermentation runs in a pressurised tank, and the wine is filtered and bottled young, keeping its primary fruit: Prosecco (from Glera), most Lambrusco, Sekt made in volume."],
["Prosecco's map","Prosecco DOC spans Veneto and Friuli; the hills hold the DOCGs, Conegliano Valdobbiadene Prosecco Superiore (with Cartizze and the Rive) and Asolo. Prosecco DOC Rosé, allowed since 2020, adds 10 to 15% Pinot Nero and must carry a vintage."],
["Ancestral / pét-nat","One fermentation, bottled before it finishes, so the last of the sugar makes the bubbles; often crown-capped and sometimes cloudy. The oldest method: Limoux claims 1531, and Blanquette de Limoux Méthode Ancestrale, Clairette de Die and Bugey Cerdon keep it in their rules."],
["Crémant map","Alsace, Bourgogne, Loire, Jura, Limoux, Die, Savoie and Bordeaux: traditional method, local grapes, at least 9 months on the lees and 12 before release."],
["The minimums","Champagne non-vintage 15 months from tirage (12 on the lees), vintage 3 years. Cava de Guarda 9 months; Cava de Guarda Superior, created in 2020, covers Reserva 18, Gran Reserva 30 and Paraje Calificado 36. Franciacorta 18 months on the lees, Satèn and Rosé 24, Millesimato 30, Riserva 60."],
["Sweetness words","Brut Nature 0 to 3 g/L, Extra Brut 0 to 6, Brut under 12, Extra Dry 12 to 17, Sec 17 to 32, Demi-Sec 32 to 50, Doux over 50."],
["England & elsewhere","The chalk of Sussex, Kent and Hampshire belongs to the same Anglo-Paris basin as Champagne's (Nyetimber, Ridgeview), planted to Champagne's grapes; Tasmania, California's Anderson Valley and Trentodoc round out the serious set."],
["Reading pressure","Fully sparkling wine runs at about 5 to 6 atmospheres in Champagne (the EU minimum is 3 bar); semi-sparkling (frizzante, pétillant, Perlwein) at 1 to 2.5. Spumante against frizzante, mousseux against pétillant, appear as label questions."]],
traps:[
"Pét-nat is one fermentation: never call it a second-fermentation method.",
"Most Prosecco, DOCG included, is made in tank: the quality tier names a place, not a method.",
"Cava's 9-month minimum is the lowest of the big three traditional-method wines, and the numbers get swapped on papers.",
"Asti and Moscato d'Asti come from one interrupted fermentation in tank: neither traditional method nor standard Charmat.",
"Extra Dry is sweeter than Brut: the words run the wrong way to an English ear."]},

{cat:"Fortified Wines",
lead:"Wine plus spirit, four great schools: Sherry's flor and solera in Jerez, Port's sweetness stopped by grape spirit in the Douro, Madeira's deliberately heated near-immortality, and the vins doux naturels of the French south, with Rutherglen's stickies alongside.",
exam:"Sherry styles from dry to sweet and the flor logic, solera mechanics, Port's categories (Vintage, LBV, Tawny, Colheita) and grapes, Madeira's noble grapes and heating, VDN basics.",
facts:[
["Sherry, biological","Palomino on chalk-white albariza. Fino and Manzanilla (Sanlúcar's salty seaside version) age under a blanket of flor yeast at about 15%: bone-dry, almond, bread dough. Under the rules agreed in 2021 they need no fortifying if they reach that strength naturally, and ageing may take place anywhere in the Marco de Jerez, though Manzanilla still ages only in Sanlúcar."],
["Sherry, oxidative","Oloroso is fortified to about 17%, past flor's limit, and ages in contact with air: walnut, toffee, dry. Amontillado begins under flor and finishes oxidatively; Palo Cortado has an Amontillado's nose and an Oloroso's body, the elegant in-between."],
["Sherry, sweet","Pedro Ximénez (PX): black and figgy, often 400 g/L of sugar and more; Moscatel; Cream is sweetened Oloroso, Pale Cream a sweetened Fino. VOS and VORS certify an average age of 20 and 30 years; 12- and 15-year statements exist too."],
["Solera","Fractional blending: wine for bottling is drawn from the oldest tier (the solera itself), which is refilled from the first criadera, that from the next, and so on. The blend's average age settles over the years; vintage Sherry (añada) is a rare exception."],
["Port","Douro schist; Touriga Nacional, Touriga Franca, Tinta Roriz, Tinta Barroca and Tinto Cão among many grapes. Fermentation is stopped about halfway with grape spirit (aguardente) of about 77%, leaving the wine sweet at around 19 to 22%; the lodges of Vila Nova de Gaia traditionally aged it."],
["Port styles","Ruby and Reserve (young, fruity); LBV (one year, bottled between the fourth and sixth year, ready to drink); Vintage (declared years only, bottled after two to three years in wood, aged for decades in bottle, decanted); Single Quinta Vintage; Tawny with an indication of age (10, 20, 30, 40 years: oxidative, nutty, silky); Colheita (a single-year tawny, at least seven years in wood); white and rosé Port."],
["Madeira","Tinta Negra is the workhorse; the noble grapes run from dry to sweet, Sercial, Verdelho, Bual, Malmsey, with rare Terrantez. Heated in tanks (estufagem) or warmed slowly in casks under the lodge roofs (canteiro): already oxidised, and so nearly indestructible once open. Age words: 5, 10 and 15 years and older; Colheita, a single harvest; Frasqueira, at least 20 years in cask."],
["VDN & Rutherglen","Vins doux naturels: neutral spirit added during fermentation (mutage). White from Muscat (Beaumes-de-Venise, Rivesaltes, Frontignan), red from Grenache (Banyuls, Maury, Rasteau). Rutherglen Muscat and Topaque (Muscadelle) climb Australia's raisined ladder: Rutherglen, Classic, Grand, Rare."],
["Other fortified","Marsala from Sicily (Oro and Ambra from white grapes such as Grillo, Catarratto and Inzolia, Rubino from red; Fine, Superiore and Vergine by age), and Portugal's Moscatel de Setúbal."]],
traps:[
"Fino is fortified low (about 15%) so flor lives; Oloroso high (about 17%) so it dies: the logic is the answer.",
"Manzanilla is Fino-style Sherry aged in Sanlúcar de Barrameda: a place, not a grape.",
"Vintage Port needs decanting; aged Tawny does not: a service question favourite.",
"Madeira's noble grape ladder runs dry to sweet: Sercial, Verdelho, Bual, Malmsey.",
"Colheita Port is a tawny from one year, not a Vintage Port: aged in wood and ready on release."]},

{cat:"Sweet Wines",
lead:"Four honest routes to sweetness: noble rot's shrivelling (Sauternes, Tokaji, Beerenauslese and Trockenbeerenauslese), drying the grapes (passito, Vin Santo, straw wine), freezing them on the vine (Eiswein, Icewine), or stopping the fermentation. Each method leaves fingerprints the exam expects you to read.",
exam:"Method to wine above all: botrytis wines against dried-grape wines against ice wines; the great names' grapes; the German Prädikat sweet tiers; Sauternes and Barsac; Tokaji's terms; the balance of sugar and acid.",
facts:[
["Noble rot (botrytis)","Botrytis cinerea needs misty mornings and dry afternoons: it pierces the skins, concentrates sugar and acid together, and adds saffron, honey and ginger. Sauternes (Sémillon with Sauvignon Blanc), Tokaji Aszú (Furmint), German and Austrian Beerenauslese and Trockenbeerenauslese, Quarts de Chaume and Bonnezeaux (Chenin), Alsace Sélection de Grains Nobles."],
["Sauternes & Barsac","Five communes, Sauternes, Barsac, Bommes, Fargues and Preignac, where the cool Ciron meets the warmer Garonne and breeds autumn mist; Barsac may label under either name. In 1855 Château d'Yquem alone was ranked Premier Cru Supérieur."],
["Tokaji","Aszú berries are steeped in base wine or must; since 2013 Aszú needs at least 120 g/L of sugar, and puttonyos, if shown, read 5 or 6. Eszencia, the free-run syrup, carries 450 g/L and more at only a few per cent alcohol; Szamorodni is the whole-bunch wine, dry or sweet."],
["German & Austrian tiers","Auslese (often sweet, sometimes botrytised), Beerenauslese (botrytised berries), Eiswein (frozen grapes), Trockenbeerenauslese (shrivelled, botrytised berries, the richest of all). Austria adds Strohwein, from grapes dried on straw or reed, and keeps Ausbruch for Rust alone as Ruster Ausbruch DAC, at TBA must weight."],
["Dried grapes","Appassimento or passito: Recioto di Soave and Recioto della Valpolicella, Vin Santo (Trebbiano and Malvasia, aged oxidatively in small sealed caratelli), Santorini's Vinsanto, the vin de paille of the Jura, and Jurançon's passerillage on the vine."],
["Frozen grapes","Eiswein and Icewine: healthy grapes frozen on the vine (about minus 7°C in Germany, minus 8°C under Canada's VQA) and pressed while frozen, for pure fruit and searing acid with no botrytis spice."],
["Stopped or fortified sweet","Moscato d'Asti (fermentation arrested by chilling) against VDNs and Port (arrested by spirit): light aromatic sweetness against powerful fortified sweetness."],
["The balance law","Great sweet wine tastes vibrant, not cloying, because acidity carries the sugar: Mosel Trockenbeerenauslese at 7% alcohol, Tokaji's Furmint spine, Sauternes' Sauvignon lift."],
["Service","Well chilled, in small pours (2 to 3 oz, 60 to 90 ml), with or as dessert; the wine must be sweeter than the dish, or it turns thin and bitter by contrast."]],
traps:[
"Eiswein carries no botrytis character: saffron and marmalade point to Beerenauslese or Trockenbeerenauslese instead.",
"Vin Santo ferments and ages for years in small sealed barrels: oxidative by design, not a flaw.",
"Moscato d'Asti is sweet from arrested fermentation, not added sugar: its low alcohol, about 5 to 6%, is the tell.",
"Tokaji Aszú's puttonyos now read 5 or 6 only; Eszencia stands outside the scale.",
"Barsac may be sold as Sauternes, never the other way round."]},

{cat:"Terroir: Climate & Soil",
lead:"The examiner's favourite 'why': climate classes (maritime, continental, Mediterranean), the moderators (water, fog, altitude, slope), and the famous soils, Kimmeridgian, chalk, terra rossa, galets, llicorella and slate, each tied to one region and one explanation.",
exam:"Match soil to region to effect; classify a region's climate; explain diurnal shift, rain shadow, fog and altitude; growing degree days; the 30° to 50° latitude bands.",
facts:[
["Climate classes","Maritime (ocean-buffered, mild, a risk of rain: Bordeaux, Rías Baixas). Continental (marked seasons: Burgundy, the Mosel, Ribera del Duero). Mediterranean (warm dry summers: the Midi, most of California, South Australia)."],
["Measuring heat","Growing degree days add up each day's mean temperature above 10°C (50°F) over the season; the Winkler scale from UC Davis sorts California from Region I (cool) to Region V (hot)."],
["Moderators","Water tempers (the Finger Lakes, Niagara, the Gironde beside the Médoc); fog cools (Russian River, Casablanca, Monterey); altitude trades heat for sunlight and a wide day-night swing (Uco, Cafayate, Etna); slope and aspect catch the sun and shed frost (the Mosel's steep south-facing slate)."],
["Rain shadows","Mountains wring out the storms: the Cascades (eastern Washington's desert), the Vosges (Alsace's dryness), the Andes (Mendoza)."],
["Limestone family","Kimmeridgian marl with its tiny oyster fossils (Chablis, Sancerre, the Aube's Côte des Bar); chalk (Champagne, the English South Downs); tuffeau (the Loire, its cellars cut into the rock); albariza (Jerez), a white marl rich in diatoms."],
["Gravel & clay","Left Bank Bordeaux's warm, free-draining gravel ripens Cabernet Sauvignon; the Right Bank's cooler clay and limestone suit Merlot: the soil explains the blend."],
["Volcanic, granite & schist","Etna's ash, Santorini's pumice, Soave Classico's basalt: smoky lift, and sandy volcanic soils where phylloxera cannot survive. Granite for the Beaujolais crus, the Northern Rhône's terraces and Dão; schist for the Douro."],
["Stones with jobs","Galets roulés store the day's heat for Grenache (Châteauneuf-du-Pape); terra rossa drains over limestone (Coonawarra); llicorella slate reflects heat and starves yields (Priorat); blue Devonian slate holds warmth for Mosel Riesling. Chianti Classico names its own: galestro (flaky clay-schist) and alberese (limestone); loess carries the Wachau and Kamptal."],
["Diurnal shift","Hot days build sugar and cold nights keep acid: the mechanism behind almost every high-altitude or desert success."]],
traps:[
"Soil chiefly manages water and heat: 'minerality you can taste from the rocks' is a belief, not chemistry. Describe the wine and credit the drainage.",
"Burgundy is continental, Bordeaux maritime: the two are contrasted constantly.",
"Albariza (Jerez) is a dazzling white marl of chalk and diatoms: it soaks up winter rain and feeds the vines through dry summers.",
"Terra rossa is red loam over limestone: the pairing matters, not only the colour.",
"Kimmeridgian links Chablis, Sancerre and the Aube, not Champagne's Côte des Blancs, which is chalk."]},

{cat:"Spirits & Cocktails",
lead:"The exam interrogates the back bar: what each spirit is made from and in which still, Cognac against Armagnac, Scotch's regions, whiskey's legal recipes, agave's split personality, and the classic cocktails you must recite by heart with glass and garnish.",
exam:"What each spirit is distilled from, the age-statement laws (VS, VSOP, XO; bourbon's new-oak rule), tequila against mezcal, and word-perfect classic recipes: Martini, Manhattan, Negroni, Old Fashioned, Margarita, Daiquiri, Sazerac.",
facts:[
["Cognac","Ugni Blanc, double distilled in copper pot stills, aged in French oak (Limousin, Tronçais): VS 2 years, VSOP 4, XO 10, counting the youngest spirit in the blend. Six crus, from Grande Champagne down through Petite Champagne, Borderies, Fins Bois, Bons Bois and Bois Ordinaires; Fine Champagne blends the top two with at least half Grande Champagne."],
["Armagnac & Calvados","Armagnac: mostly a single distillation in the column alambic armagnacais, more rustic and often vintage-dated, from Bas-Armagnac, Ténarèze and Haut-Armagnac. Calvados: apple (and pear) brandy from Normandy, double distilled in pot stills in the Pays d'Auge."],
["Whisky, old world","Scotch: at least 3 years in oak in Scotland and at least 40%; single malt is malted barley from one distillery's pot stills. The protected regions are Speyside (orchard elegance), Islay (peat and iodine), Highland (the islands fall within it), Lowland and Campbeltown. Irish: also 3 years, often triple distilled and lighter."],
["Whiskey, new world","Bourbon: at least 51% corn, new charred oak containers, distilled to no more than 80% and nothing added but water, made anywhere in the United States; 'straight' means at least 2 years. Tennessee whiskey is filtered through maple charcoal before ageing (the Lincoln County Process). Rye: at least 51% rye, and spicier."],
["Agave","Tequila: Blue Weber agave, at least 51% (100% agave for the good stuff), from Jalisco and named parts of four other states; Blanco, Reposado (2 to 12 months in oak), Añejo (1 to 3 years), Extra Añejo (3 years and more). Mezcal: many agaves, roasted in earth pits (hence the smoke), with Oaxaca at its heart."],
["Clear & botanical","Vodka: neutral by definition. Gin: juniper first; London Dry means nothing but water and a trace of sugar added after distillation. Rum: molasses or sugar cane; rhum agricole, from fresh cane juice, is the style of the French islands, with an AOC in Martinique."],
["Pomace, pisco & vermouth","Grappa (Italy) and marc (France) are distilled from pomace, the skins and seeds left after pressing. Pisco is grape brandy from Peru (never aged in wood) and Chile. Vermouth is wine fortified and flavoured with botanicals, wormwood by tradition, dry or sweet; it fades once open, so keep it in the fridge."],
["Stirred classics","Martini (gin and dry vermouth, olive or lemon twist), Manhattan (rye or bourbon, sweet vermouth, Angostura, cherry), Negroni (gin, Campari and sweet vermouth in equal parts, orange), Old Fashioned (whiskey, sugar, bitters, orange peel), Sazerac (rye, sugar, Peychaud's bitters, an absinthe-rinsed glass, lemon peel)."],
["Shaken classics","Daiquiri (rum, lime, sugar), Margarita (tequila, lime, orange liqueur, salt rim), Sidecar (Cognac, lemon, Cointreau, often a sugar rim), Whiskey Sour (whiskey, lemon, sugar, egg white optional). Shake anything with citrus, juice, cream or egg; stir spirits alone."]],
traps:[
"Bourbon needs new charred oak every time: its used barrels go on to Scotch, rum and tequila around the world.",
"Cognac and Armagnac age statements count the youngest spirit in the blend.",
"In exam terms a Martini is made with gin unless vodka is asked for, and 'dry' means less vermouth.",
"Shake or stir has a reason: dilution, chill and texture for juice; silk and clarity for spirits alone.",
"Bourbon need not come from Kentucky: any American state may make it."]},

{cat:"Beer & Cider",
lead:"The other fermentations a sommelier must pour with confidence: ale against lager yeast at the root of every beer style, the classic families from Pilsner to stout, Belgium's wild and monastic traditions, and cider's own appellations from Normandy to Asturias.",
exam:"Ale (top-fermenting, warm, quick) against lager (bottom-fermenting, cold, slow); the four ingredients; style to country; Trappist against abbey; lambic and gueuze; dry against sweet cider; proper service.",
facts:[
["Four ingredients","Water, malted grain (sugar, colour and body), hops (bitterness, aroma and preservation; bitterness is measured in IBU) and yeast. Bavaria's Reinheitsgebot of 1516 allowed only water, barley and hops, before yeast was understood."],
["The great divide","Ales: Saccharomyces cerevisiae, warm and quick, with fruity esters (IPA, stout, wheat beer, most Belgians). Lagers: S. pastorianus, cold and slow, with clean lines (Pilsner, Helles, bock, Vienna lager)."],
["Pilsner's birthplace","Plzeň, Bohemia, 1842 (Pilsner Urquell): soft water and Saaz hops, the template for the world's pale lager."],
["Britain & Ireland","Cask bitter and ESB, porter (London's original), dry Irish stout (Guinness, poured on a nitrogen mix for its creamy cascade), barley wine."],
["Belgium","Trappist beer is brewed within or beside a Trappist abbey under the monks' oversight, its profit going to the community or to charity: Chimay, Orval, Rochefort, Westmalle and Westvleteren in Belgium. Dubbel, tripel and quadrupel; saison's peppery farmhouse style; witbier with coriander and orange peel."],
["Wild Brussels","Lambic: spontaneous fermentation by wild yeast and bacteria, the wort cooled overnight in open coolships in the Senne valley and Pajottenland around Brussels. Gueuze blends young and old lambic and referments in the bottle; kriek adds cherries, framboise raspberries; the oude classics are tart and never sweetened."],
["Germany's calendar","Hefeweizen (banana and clove from its yeast), Kölsch (Cologne's top-fermented, cold-conditioned hybrid), Märzen and Oktoberfest beer, Rauchbier (Bamberg's smoked malt), Doppelbock (the monks' 'liquid bread')."],
["Cider","Normandy and Brittany: bittersweet apples, often keeved, gently sweet and richly appley (AOP Pays d'Auge and Cornouaille, Poiré Domfront for perry, with Pommeau and Calvados alongside); England's West Country: tannic and dry; Asturias and the Basque Country: sharp, still sidra, poured from a height to aerate it."]],
traps:[
"Trappist is a protected label policed by the International Trappist Association; 'abbey' beer is a style any brewery may make.",
"Lambic ferments spontaneously with wild airborne yeast: no pitched yeast is the whole point.",
"Stout's blackness is roasted barley, yet dry Irish stout is often light in body and alcohol, and it pairs with oysters: a service classic.",
"Serve most craft ales cool, at cellar temperature (45 to 55°F, 7 to 13°C): ice-cold kills the aromatics, another service question.",
"Kölsch is top-fermented but cold-conditioned like a lager: a hybrid, not a lager."]},

{cat:"Sake",
lead:"Rice, water, koji and yeast: sake is brewed by multiple parallel fermentation, never distilled, and its ladder is milling. The more of each grain polished away, the higher the grade, from Honjozo and Junmai up to Daiginjo, with the aromatic Ginjo styles served cool, not hot.",
exam:"The special-designation grades and seimaibuai (polishing ratios), Junmai against added-alcohol styles, koji's job, the Nigori, Nama, Genshu and Kimoto terms, the sake meter value, service temperature by style, food pairing logic.",
facts:[
["What sake is","Brewed from polished rice: koji mould (Aspergillus oryzae) turns starch into sugar while yeast turns that sugar into alcohol in the same tank, multiple parallel fermentation, the defining technique. About 18 to 20% undiluted, usually brought down to around 15 to 16%."],
["The polish ladder","Seimaibuai is the share of each grain that remains. Honjozo: 70% or less. Ginjo: 60% or less. Daiginjo: 50% or less. Junmai has had no minimum since 2004 but must state its ratio. More polish brings more aromatic finesse and a higher price."],
["Junmai vs Honjozo","Junmai means rice, water, koji and yeast only. Honjozo, and Ginjo and Daiginjo without the word Junmai, add a measured dose of distilled alcohol (capped at 10% of the polished rice's weight) for lift and fragrance: a style choice, not adulteration. Tokubetsu ('special') marks extra polish or a special method."],
["Style terms","Nigori: coarsely filtered, cloudy, often gently sweet. Nama: unpasteurised, keep it cold. Genshu: undiluted, 18 to 20%. Koshu: aged. Kimoto and Yamahai: slow, traditional yeast starters with lactic depth. Taruzake: rested in cedar casks. Futsushu: ordinary table grade. Sparkling sake is a growing category."],
["Reading the label","The sake meter value (Nihonshu-do) rises with dryness: plus numbers are drier, minus numbers sweeter. Acidity, the polishing ratio, the rice variety and the brewery's prefecture often appear too."],
["Rice & water","Yamada Nishiki is the king of sake rice, with Omachi and Gohyakumangoku beside it. Water matters: Nada's hard water ferments robust sake, Fushimi's soft water gentler sake."],
["Service","Premium Ginjo and Daiginjo: chilled or cool, in a wine glass, since heat flattens their aromatics. Sturdy Junmai and Honjozo take gentle warmth (nurukan, about 40°C); hot sake (atsukan, about 50°C) suits rustic grades and cold nights."],
["At the table","Umami meets umami with no tannin to clash: sake flatters miso, mushrooms, shellfish and fried food, the sommelier's secret weapon for 'impossible' pairings."]],
traps:[
"Seimaibuai counts what remains: Daiginjo at 50% means at least half of each grain was polished away.",
"Do not heat Daiginjo: 'sake is served hot' is the paper's planted misconception.",
"Junmai means no added alcohol; Honjozo's small addition is traditional technique, not a fault.",
"Sake is brewed from grain, as beer is, and never distilled: 'rice wine' is a misnomer, since wine is fermented fruit.",
"A plus sake meter value means drier, not sweeter."]},

{cat:"Wine Fundamentals",
lead:"The bedrock chapter: what wine is, the noble grapes' core profiles, how to read structure (acid, tannin, alcohol, sweetness, body), Old World against New World instincts, and the vocabulary (vintage, cuvée, terroir, balance) every later chapter leans on.",
exam:"Grape to profile identification, structure defined and assessed, the deductive grid's order, white against red winemaking at a glance, standard bottle arithmetic, basic label literacy.",
facts:[
["Structure, defined","Acidity: mouthwatering freshness. Tannin: drying grip from skins, seeds, stems and oak (mostly in reds). Alcohol: warmth and body. Sweetness: residual sugar. Body: the weight of the whole. Balance is the peace treaty between them."],
["The tasting grid","Sight (clarity, concentration, colour, rim), then nose (fruit, then non-fruit, earth and wood), then palate (sweetness, acid, tannin, alcohol, body, finish, confirming the nose), then a conclusion: Old or New World, climate, grape, country, age range. The exam's blind tasting asks for this order."],
["The big whites","Chardonnay: a chameleon, apple to tropical, with a fondness for oak and malolactic. Sauvignon Blanc: pungent herbs and citrus, rarely oaked. Riesling: high acid, floral and petrol, any sweetness, rarely oaked. Chenin Blanc, Pinot Gris, Viognier and Gewurztraminer fill out the aromatic range."],
["The big reds","Cabernet Sauvignon: cassis and cedar, high tannin. Merlot: plum, supple. Pinot Noir: red fruit, silky, pale, high acid. Syrah: pepper, meat, black fruit. Grenache: raspberry and spice, warm. Sangiovese, Nebbiolo and Tempranillo: the Europeans of acid and structure."],
["Colour comes from skins","Most grape juice runs clear. Red wine ferments on its skins, usually warm (about 25 to 30°C) with punching down or pumping over; white is pressed off its skins and fermented cooler; rosé takes a brief skin contact, or, in Champagne alone, a blend of red and white wine."],
["Bottle maths","750 ml pours five 5 oz (150 ml) glasses, or about six 4 oz tastes; a case is 12 bottles; a magnum is 1.5 litres. The Business of the Sommelier chapter builds on this arithmetic."],
["Reading a label","Producer, appellation, grape (or not: much of Europe names the place only), vintage, alcohol. Reserva and Riserva carry legal minimums in Spain and Italy; 'Réserve' and 'Reserve' mean nothing in law in France or the United States."],
["Old vs New instincts","Old World: named for the place, earthier, a tradition of higher acid. New World: named for the grape, a tradition of riper fruit. These are instincts, not laws, and climate change and style blur both."]],
traps:[
"Tannin is not acidity: grip on the gums against water on the tongue. Mixing them up fails tasting answers.",
"'Dry' means no sugar, not no fruit: ripe fruit aroma is not sweetness.",
"Vintage is the harvest year, never the bottling year.",
"Rosé by blending red and white wine is forbidden for still wine in Europe; Champagne's rosé is the exception that proves the rule."]},

{cat:"Classifications & Labels",
lead:"The lawyer's chapter: Europe's PDO and PGI pyramid and each country's dialect of it (AOP, DOCG and DOC, DOCa and DO, the Prädikat and the VDP, Grand Cru's several meanings) against the New World's appellations, which draw borders and little else.",
exam:"Rank the tiers country by country; what each abbreviation guarantees; where 'Grand Cru' means a vineyard (Burgundy, Alsace), a village (Champagne) or a château (Saint-Émilion, the 1855 ranking); the percentage rules nation by nation.",
facts:[
["EU pyramid","PDO (AOP, DOP, g.U.): protected origin, tight rules. PGI (IGP, IGT): looser and regional. Plain wine below. Every national system nests into this."],
["France","AOP (still often written AOC), then IGP, then Vin de France. The cru words change meaning: Grand Cru and Premier Cru name vineyards in Burgundy and Alsace, whole villages in Champagne, châteaux in Saint-Émilion's revisable classification, and estates in the fixed 1855 ranking of the Médoc and Sauternes."],
["Italy","DOCG (guaranteed, sealed with a numbered government strip on the neck), then DOC, then IGT, then Vino. Classico marks the historic heart; Superiore, higher ripeness or alcohol; Riserva, longer ageing with legal minimums set by each DOC."],
["Spain","DOCa (DOQ in Catalan: Rioja and Priorat only) and single-estate Vino de Pago at the top, then DO, then Vino de la Tierra. The ageing words are law: a red Rioja Crianza spends 2 years with 1 in oak, a Reserva 3 with 1 in oak, a Gran Reserva 5 with 2 in oak."],
["Germany","The Prädikat ladder ranks ripeness by must weight (Kabinett, Spätlese, Auslese, Beerenauslese, Eiswein, Trockenbeerenauslese), with Qualitätswein beneath it. The 2021 wine law adds an origin ladder, binding from the 2026 vintage: growing region, then a smaller region, then village (Ortswein), then single site (Lagenwein). The VDP's private pyramid (Gutswein, Ortswein, Erste Lage, Grosse Lage, with Grosses Gewächs for the dry top) inspired it and still overlays it."],
["USA & New World","AVA, GI and WO names draw borders only. The United States: 85% from a named AVA (75% for a county or state, 100% for California), 75% for a variety (Oregon 90% for most grapes), 95% for a vintage with an AVA, 85% without. Australia, New Zealand and South Africa: 85% for each claim. Chile: 75%, or 85% for the European Union."],
["Estate words","Mis en bouteille au château or au domaine, 'Estate Bottled', Gutsabfüllung: grown and bottled by the producer. These are the words with teeth, against the puffery of an American 'Reserve', 'Old Vine' or 'Proprietor's Blend'."]],
traps:[
"'Reserve' is legally binding in Spain and Italy, decorative in France and the United States.",
"Champagne's crus rate whole villages; Burgundy's rate single vineyards: the same word at a different scale.",
"DOCG's guarantee shows on the bottle: the numbered neck strip.",
"An AVA tells you where, never what or how: no grape or yield rules."]},

{cat:"Viticulture & Winemaking",
lead:"From budbreak to bottling: the vine's year, the diseases and the phylloxera solution, harvest chemistry, then the cellar's forks in the road (fermentation, malolactic, oak, lees) and the special methods (carbonic, appassimento, sur lie) that mark whole styles.",
exam:"Phylloxera and grafting, the order of the growing season, Brix and potential alcohol, what malolactic does, new against used oak, carbonic maceration, clarification and stability, the common faults (TCA, Brett, VA, oxidation).",
facts:[
["The vine's year","Budbreak, flowering, fruit set, véraison (the colour change: acid falls, sugar climbs), harvest, dormancy. The northern hemisphere picks from August to October, the southern from February to April."],
["Phylloxera","An American root louse that devastated Europe from the 1860s; the cure is grafting vinifera onto American rootstock. Chile, South Australia, sandy soils and a few other pockets (parts of Washington) escaped it."],
["Vineyard choices","Training (bush vine or gobelet, Guyot cane pruning, cordon spur pruning, pergola) and canopy management trade shade against sun; low yields concentrate; organic, biodynamic and sustainable are farming philosophies; dry farming drives the roots deep."],
["Fermentation maths","Yeast turns sugar into alcohol and carbon dioxide; roughly 17 g/L of sugar, about 1.7 to 1.8 degrees Brix, makes 1% alcohol. Stop it early for sweetness, fortify to arrest it, or let it run dry."],
["Malolactic","A bacterial conversion of tart malic acid into soft lactic acid: standard for reds, a style choice for Chardonnay (the buttery note is diacetyl), blocked with sulphur dioxide or cold for crisp whites."],
["Oak decisions","New barrels give vanilla, spice and toast (French oak a subtler spice, American oak coconut and dill); used barrels give oxygen without flavour; size scales the effect (the Bordeaux barrique of 225 litres, the Burgundy pièce of 228, the large neutral foudre); alternatives include chips, staves, concrete, amphora and steel."],
["Special methods","Carbonic maceration (whole berries under carbon dioxide: the banana and bubblegum of Beaujolais's semi-carbonic wines), appassimento (dried grapes), sur lie with bâtonnage (a creamy texture), skin-contact whites (orange wine)."],
["Clarity & stability","Sulphur dioxide guards against oxidation and microbes. Fining (bentonite, egg white, isinglass, casein) and filtration clarify, which is why some wines are not vegan. Cold stabilisation drops tartrate crystals, the harmless 'wine diamonds' a guest may mistake for glass."],
["Faults","TCA, 'corked' (wet cardboard, a muted wine); Brettanomyces (sticking plaster, farmyard); volatile acidity (vinegar, nail varnish); oxidation (bruised apple, browning); reduction (struck match, rotten egg, often blown off by air)."]],
traps:[
"Véraison is the colour change in midsummer, not the harvest.",
"Malolactic is bacterial, not yeast, and converts an acid: it is not a second alcoholic fermentation.",
"Grafting onto American roots saved Europe: hybrids did not, and vinifera stayed the fruit.",
"A 'corked' wine means TCA taint, never bits of cork floating in the glass."]},

{cat:"Wine Trade & Aging",
lead:"The commerce-and-cellar chapter: how wine reaches the list (America's three-tier system, négociants and La Place de Bordeaux, en primeur), what ages and why, the physics of a good cellar, the great closure debate, and the bottle sizes and auction words of the collector's market.",
exam:"Three-tier roles, how en primeur works, what makes a wine age-worthy, cellar conditions by number, cork against screwcap, bottle sizes, ullage and provenance vocabulary.",
facts:[
["Three-tier (USA)","Producer or importer, then distributor, then retailer or restaurant: a legacy of Prohibition. The sommelier buys from distributors, and the markups stack accordingly."],
["Bordeaux's system","Châteaux sell through négociants, brokered by courtiers, on La Place de Bordeaux; en primeur means buying in the spring after the harvest, while the wine is still in barrel, with delivery about two years later."],
["What ages","Wines with structure to spend: high acid, tannin, concentration, sweetness or fortification, and above all balance. Most wine is made for drinking now; ageability is the exception sold as the rule."],
["The ageing curve","Fruit fades and tertiary notes rise (leather, forest floor, dried fruit, nuts); colours converge, whites darkening towards gold and reds paling towards garnet and brick; tannins knit and fall as sediment."],
["Cellar physics","About 55°F (13°C) and steady, around 70% humidity, dark and still, bottles under natural cork on their sides. Heat and light are the killers, and wide swings in temperature do damage of their own."],
["Closures","Natural cork: tradition, a trickle of oxygen, a risk of TCA. Screwcap: reliability and freshness, standard in New Zealand and Australia. Technical corks such as Diam, cleaned of TCA: the middle path. The debate is about style, not morals."],
["Formats","Half bottle 375 ml, bottle 750 ml, magnum 1.5 litres. In Champagne: Jeroboam 3 litres, Rehoboam 4.5, Methuselah 6, Salmanazar 9, Balthazar 12, Nebuchadnezzar 15. In Bordeaux: double magnum 3 litres, Jeroboam 5, Imperial 6. A magnum ages more slowly than a bottle and is prized for it."],
["Auction talk","Ullage, the fill level, is evidence of provenance: into the neck is good, top shoulder normal for an old bottle, mid shoulder a warning. OWC means original wooden case; wine released ex-château, straight from the property, commands trust."]],
traps:[
"En primeur is paying now for wine not yet bottled: a bet on price, not early drinking.",
"Magnums age more slowly (less oxygen per measure of wine): the big-bottle premium is physics plus rarity.",
"The three-tier system is American law, not a worldwide rule.",
"High alcohol alone does not age a wine; balance and structure do, and plenty of 15% wines die young."]},

{cat:"Service & Hospitality",
lead:"The floor exam in written form: the order of service, opening still and sparkling wine correctly, when and how to decant, temperatures by style, the logic of glassware, and the hospitality judgement calls: refusals, corked bottles, allergies, and the guest who is always right until the law says otherwise.",
exam:"Exact service sequences and temperatures, who is served when, presenting the cork, handling a rejected or faulty bottle, the order of wines, pour counts, the triggers for responsible service.",
facts:[
["The sequence","Present the label to the host and confirm the wine aloud; cut the foil below the lip; wipe; draw the cork quietly; place it to the host's right; pour the host a taste; once it is approved, serve the guests clockwise from their right (traditionally women first, though many houses now simply go round the table), the host last; set the bottle down with its label to the host."],
["Glasses & order","Glasses polished, carried by the stem, set to the right above the knife; a flute or tulip for sparkling, a larger bowl for reds. Wines run white before red, light before full, dry before sweet, young before old."],
["Sparkling discipline","Thumb on the cork from the moment the cage loosens (six half-turns) until the cork is out; the bottle at 30 to 45 degrees; turn the bottle, not the cork; a sigh, not a pop; pour in two stages."],
["Decanting","Old wine for its sediment (stand the bottle upright for a day, then pour in one steady movement over a light). Young tannic wine for air. Fragile old Pinot Noir is often better left alone. Present the original bottle either way."],
["Temperatures","Sparkling 42 to 50°F (6 to 10°C); light whites and rosé 45 to 50°F (7 to 10°C); full whites 50 to 55°F (10 to 13°C); light reds 55 to 60°F (13 to 16°C); full reds 60 to 65°F (16 to 18°C); Fino well chilled, Vintage Port like a full red."],
["The corked-bottle rite","When a guest rejects a faulty wine, apologise without argument, remove the glasses, and bring a fresh bottle or the list; taste the returned bottle discreetly yourself. The tasting ritual exists to catch faults, not preferences, but hospitality usually absorbs both."],
["Pour maths on the floor","A taste is about 1 oz (30 ml); a glass 5 to 6 oz; five glasses to the bottle. At a full pour a table of eight needs two bottles for one round; at about 3 oz each, one bottle goes round eight. Count before the host has to."],
["Responsible service","Visible intoxication ends service: the law and the licence outrank the guest. Offer food, water, coffee or a taxi, with quiet firmness."]],
traps:[
"The host tastes first but is poured last: approval, then hospitality.",
"Never sniff the cork yourself at the table; present it. The guest may look at it; the sommelier reads the wine.",
"White wine service is not 'as cold as possible': over-chilling is a scored error.",
"A guest who dislikes a sound wine is a hospitality decision; a faulty wine is an obligation. Know which conversation you are in."]},

{cat:"Food & Pairing",
lead:"The dining room's logic: weight with weight, acid with acid, the wine sweeter than the dessert, salt to soften tannin, and care with heat and bitterness; then the canonical marriages (oysters and Muscadet, foie gras and Sauternes, Stilton and Port) that are asked word for word.",
exam:"The principles stated as rules, then recall of the classic pairings; what fights wine (artichoke, asparagus, chilli heat, vinegar) and what rescues it (salt, fat, acid in the dish).",
facts:[
["First principles","Match intensity (delicate sole is no match for Barolo); an acidic dish wants an acidic wine; the wine must be sweeter than the dessert; salt softens tannin and loves acid; fat wants acid to cut it or tannin to scrub it."],
["Tannin's rules","Tannin with protein and fat: harmony (steak and Cabernet). Tannin with oily fish: a metallic clash. Tannin with chilli heat: fire amplified. Tannin with salt: softened."],
["Pair the strongest element","The sauce, the cooking method and the seasoning often matter more than the protein: chicken in a cream sauce wants a different wine from chicken in red wine."],
["The canon","Oysters with Muscadet, Chablis or Champagne; goat's cheese with Sancerre; foie gras with Sauternes; duck with Pinot Noir; lamb with Bordeaux or Rioja; cassoulet with Madiran; choucroute with Alsace Riesling; Stilton with Port; Roquefort with Sauternes; chocolate with Banyuls."],
["Regional reflex","'What grows together goes together' is a safe exam answer: Sancerre and chèvre, Chianti and bistecca, Albariño and shellfish, Muscadet and oysters."],
["Problem foods","Artichoke (its cynarin makes wine taste falsely sweet), asparagus, raw vinegar, fierce chilli, egg yolk: answer with high-acid, unoaked, often off-dry whites, or with sparkling wine; sweetness cools capsaicin."],
["Cheese truths","White wine beats red with most cheese (acid against fat, and no fight between tannin and salt); blue cheese with sweet wine is the great law; hard aged cheeses can carry reds."],
["Umami & bubbles","Umami-rich food (mushrooms, soy, aged cheese) makes tannin taste harder: sparkling wine and sake are the fix, and Champagne with anything fried is the sommelier's cheat code."]],
traps:[
"A sweet wine must exceed the dessert's sweetness, or it tastes thin and sour.",
"A big red with delicate fish is the classic scored error: a weight mismatch plus the clash of tannin and fish oil.",
"Chilli heat is amplified by alcohol and tannin: reach for off-dry Riesling, not Shiraz.",
"'Red with all cheese' is folklore; the exam rewards the reasoning behind white with cheese."]},

{cat:"Business of the Sommelier",
lead:"The arithmetic that keeps the cellar open: cost percentage and its inverse, markup; by-the-glass sums; cost of goods, inventory turns and variance; list architecture and pricing psychology; and the buying calendar. The exam does this with real numbers, so drill the formulas.",
exam:"Compute cost percentage (cost divided by price), a price from a target cost percentage, a glass price from bottle cost, margin against markup, cost of goods sold, inventory value and variance, sizes and yields.",
facts:[
["The master formula","Beverage cost % = cost divided by selling price, times 100. For a price at a target: cost divided by the target percentage gives the list price. A $12 bottle at a 25% target lists at $48."],
["Glass maths","A 750 ml bottle pours five 5 oz glasses. The classic rule prices one glass at the bottle's wholesale cost, so the first glass pays for the bottle and the pour runs at a 20% cost. By-the-glass wines carry that lower cost percentage to cover the waste of open bottles, and they drive volume."],
["Margin vs markup","Markup multiplies cost (3 × a $12 bottle = $36); margin is profit as a percentage of the price ($24 of $36 = a 67% margin, which is a 33% cost). The exam swaps these to catch you."],
["Cost of goods","Cost of goods sold = opening inventory plus purchases minus closing inventory; divide it by the period's sales for the true cost percentage."],
["Inventory discipline","Value inventory at cost. Turnover = annual cost of goods sold divided by average inventory, and a list that turns several times a year keeps its cash working. Variance = the physical count against what the till says was sold; shrinkage has names: overpouring, spillage, breakage, theft."],
["List architecture","A price ladder in every section, anchors and the psychology of the 'second-cheapest bottle', progressive markup (luxury bottles carry a lower percentage, which moves them), and the by-the-glass list as the front door."],
["Buying","Distributor representatives, allocations, pre-arrivals, closeouts; net-30 terms; case discounts and 'post-offs'. The sommelier's cellar is working capital asleep on its side."],
["Programme hygiene","Every wine keyed correctly into the point-of-sale system, storage at safe temperatures, spoilage logged, staff trained as a sales force: a well-taught server sells the second bottle."]],
traps:[
"Cost percentage and margin are complements: a 25% cost is a 75% margin. Read which one the question wants.",
"A 3 × markup is a 67% margin, not a '300% margin': vocabulary is half the battle in business questions.",
"Two glass sums are examined: the classic rule prices one glass at the bottle's wholesale cost, while the Court's BTG costing starts from the bottle's list price, divided by 25 oz (750 ml) and multiplied by the pour, so a bottle listed at $100 gives a 5 oz glass at $20.",
"Inventory is valued at what you paid, never at menu price."]}
];
